  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_56',
  'A media company needs to transfer terabytes of video data daily from its on-premises data center to Amazon S3 in an AWS VPC, requiring consistent high throughput and low latency. Which connectivity solution would be suboptimal for this primary workload?',
  'mcq',
  '["Establishing an AWS Direct Connect connection with a Link Aggregation Group (LAG).","Utilizing AWS Site-to-Site VPN as the primary connectivity method.","Implementing AWS Direct Connect with multiple dedicated connections.","Using AWS Direct Connect with a public Virtual Interface (VIF) to Amazon S3."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-002). Using AWS Site-to-Site VPN as the primary connectivity for high-throughput hybrid workloads is suboptimal because VPN is limited in bandwidth (typically ~1.25 Gbps per tunnel) and traverses the public internet, introducing variable latency and potential performance bottlenecks. Options A, C, and D, which involve AWS Direct Connect, are suitable for high-throughput, low-latency hybrid workloads due to dedicated bandwidth and consistent network performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_57',
  'A large enterprise has multiple development VPCs and a central shared services VPC containing common resources like Active Directory. How can the enterprise establish transitive routing between all development VPCs and the shared services VPC with centralized control?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure individual VPC peering connections between each development VPC and the shared services VPC.","Deploy an AWS Transit Gateway and attach all development VPCs and the shared services VPC to it.","Set up AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections from each development VPC to the shared services VPC.","Use AWS PrivateLink to connect each development VPC to specific services in the shared services VPC."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the most effective solution. Deploying an AWS Transit Gateway and attaching all VPCs creates a hub-and-spoke model, enabling transitive routing between all development VPCs and the shared services VPC with centralized route table management. Option A would lead to a complex, non-transitive mesh of peering connections. Option C is primarily for hybrid connectivity and would be overly complex for inter-VPC routing. Option D is for private service consumption, not general VPC-to-VPC routing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_58',
  'A SaaS provider hosts an application in their AWS account and wants to offer private access to their customers'' VPCs without exposing traffic to the public internet or requiring complex network configurations. Which service should the provider use?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Direct Connect","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS PrivateLink, which allows the SaaS provider to create an endpoint service that customers can connect to privately from their own VPCs using interface VPC endpoints. This eliminates the need for public IP addresses, internet gateways, VPC peering, or Direct Connect/VPN connections for service access. Option A is for general VPC connectivity. Option B is for hybrid cloud. Option D is for secure on-premises to VPC connectivity over the internet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_59',
  'A company is migrating an application to AWS that needs to support both IPv4 and IPv6 clients. The application runs on Amazon EC2 instances within a VPC. What is the recommended approach to enable both IPv4 and IPv6 connectivity for the application?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the application in a separate VPC for IPv4 and another for IPv6, then use a gateway to translate.","Configure a dual-stack architecture within the VPC, assigning both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses to resources.","Use an IPv4-only VPC and rely on NAT64 to translate all incoming IPv6 traffic to IPv4.","Implement an IPv6-only VPC and use a proxy to handle all outgoing IPv4 connections."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the recommended approach for supporting both IPv4 and IPv6 clients. A dual-stack architecture within the VPC allows resources like EC2 instances to have both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses, enabling them to communicate over both protocols. Option A introduces unnecessary complexity. Option C and Option D force a single protocol and rely on translation, which can introduce overhead and complexity, and are not the primary recommendation for native dual-stack support.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_60',
  'A large enterprise needs to connect numerous Amazon VPCs across different AWS accounts to its on-premises data centers, requiring a scalable and centrally managed network architecture. Which AWS service is specifically designed to provide a hub-and-spoke topology for this scenario, enabling transitive routing?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon VPC peering","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) is the correct choice. It is designed for connecting thousands of Amazon VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub, simplifying network management and enabling transitive routing. Option B (Amazon VPC peering) is not scalable for enterprise-scale multi-VPC connectivity as it requires N*(N-1)/2 connections for a full mesh and is non-transitive, making it an anti-pattern for this scenario. Option C (AWS Direct Connect) provides dedicated private network connections to AWS but doesn''t inherently offer the hub-and-spoke VPC connectivity model. Option D (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) connects on-premises networks to a single VPC or Transit Gateway but is not the primary service for managing a large-scale hub-and-spoke VPC network.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_61',
  'A media company is migrating its large video archives and high-resolution media processing workloads from its on-premises data center to AWS. They require a dedicated, high-bandwidth (multiple Gbps), and consistently low-latency network connection to ensure efficient data transfer and real-time processing. Which AWS service should the Solutions Architect recommend for this hybrid connectivity?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Site-to-Site VPN","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Client VPN","Amazon VPC peering"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Direct Connect) is the correct solution. It provides a dedicated, private network connection from on-premises to AWS, offering high bandwidth (up to 100 Gbps) and consistent low latency, which is ideal for high-throughput and latency-sensitive hybrid workloads like large data migrations and media processing. Option A (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) establishes an encrypted connection over the public internet, which can have variable latency and is typically limited to ~1.25 Gbps per tunnel, making it an anti-pattern for high-throughput primary connectivity. Option C (AWS Client VPN) is for remote users to access VPC resources, not for data center-to-AWS connectivity. Option D (Amazon VPC peering) connects two VPCs, not an on-premises data center to AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_62',
  'Which AWS service enables consumers in different Amazon VPCs or AWS accounts to securely access services hosted by service providers without exposing the service traffic to the public internet, simplifying network architecture and enhancing security?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon Route 53","AWS PrivateLink","Amazon VPC peering"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS PrivateLink) is the correct answer. It allows private connectivity between Amazon VPCs, AWS services, and on-premises applications without exposing data to the public internet. It creates private endpoints (interface VPC endpoints) in your VPC. Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) connects VPCs and on-premises networks but doesn''t specifically provide private access to services hosted in other VPCs without traversing shared network infrastructure. Option B (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS web service. Option D (Amazon VPC peering) connects two VPCs directly but does not provide a managed private endpoint for service consumption across accounts or VPCs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_63',
  'An enterprise has 20 Amazon VPCs spread across multiple AWS accounts and needs to establish full mesh connectivity between them for inter-application communication, while also connecting to a centralized security inspection VPC. The solution must minimize operational overhead and scale efficiently. Which networking approach should the Solutions Architect choose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a hub-and-spoke model using AWS Transit Gateway.","Create a full mesh of Amazon VPC peering connections between all VPCs.","Use AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections between each VPC.","Configure AWS Direct Connect connections from each VPC to a central hub."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Implement a hub-and-spoke model using AWS Transit Gateway) is the most appropriate solution. AWS Transit Gateway provides transitive routing, allowing all connected VPCs to communicate through a central hub, simplifying management and scaling efficiently for a large number of VPCs. This also facilitates routing traffic through a centralized security inspection VPC. Option B (Create a full mesh of Amazon VPC peering connections) is an anti-pattern for enterprise-scale multi-VPC connectivity because VPC peering is non-transitive and requires N*(N-1)/2 connections, leading to significant operational overhead and complexity for 20 VPCs. Option C (Use AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections between each VPC) is not suitable as VPNs are typically used for hybrid connectivity and would be overly complex and expensive for inter-VPC communication. Option D (Configure AWS Direct Connect connections from each VPC to a central hub) is incorrect as Direct Connect is for on-premises to AWS connectivity, not for inter-VPC routing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_64',
  'Which AWS service provides a secure, encrypted connection between a company''s on-premises network and an Amazon VPC over the public internet, suitable for general hybrid connectivity requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Site-to-Site VPN"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) is the correct answer. It establishes an encrypted tunnel over the public internet to securely connect an on-premises network to an Amazon VPC. Option A (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated private connection, not over the public internet. Option B (AWS PrivateLink) enables private access to services within AWS, not direct on-premises to VPC connectivity over the internet. Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) acts as a central hub for connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, but the direct encrypted connection over the internet is provided by a Site-to-Site VPN.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_65',
  'A global enterprise has multiple AWS accounts and Amazon VPCs deployed across several AWS Regions. They need to establish a single, highly available, and resilient private network connection from their on-premises data centers to all these AWS resources, ensuring consistent performance. Which combination of AWS services will best meet these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect and AWS Direct Connect Gateway","AWS Site-to-Site VPN and AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon VPC peering and AWS Global Accelerator","AWS PrivateLink and Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Direct Connect and AWS Direct Connect Gateway) is the correct choice. AWS Direct Connect provides dedicated private connections from on-premises to AWS. AWS Direct Connect Gateway allows you to connect your Direct Connect connection to multiple VPCs across different AWS Regions and accounts, providing a single, resilient point of entry. Option B (AWS Site-to-Site VPN and AWS Transit Gateway) could provide connectivity but VPNs traverse the public internet, offering less consistent performance and lower bandwidth than Direct Connect for primary connections. Option C (Amazon VPC peering and AWS Global Accelerator) is incorrect; VPC peering is for inter-VPC connectivity and doesn''t involve on-premises, and Global Accelerator optimizes public internet traffic. Option D (AWS PrivateLink and Amazon Route 53) is for privately accessing services within AWS or across accounts, not for primary on-premises to multi-Region AWS connectivity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_66',
  'Which AWS service allows remote users to securely access resources within an Amazon VPC and on-premises networks using a software client, providing a flexible and scalable solution for remote access?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect","AWS Client VPN","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","AWS PrivateLink"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Client VPN) is the correct answer. It is a managed client-based VPN service that enables remote employees to securely access AWS resources and on-premises networks. Option A (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated network connection from on-premises data centers to AWS, not for individual remote users. Option C (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) connects entire on-premises networks to an Amazon VPC, not individual clients. Option D (AWS PrivateLink) provides private connectivity to services within AWS, not remote user access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_1_67',
  'A large organization operates several Amazon VPCs for different business units and environments (e.g., Development, Test, Production). They need to implement a network architecture that enforces strict network segmentation, centralizes network security inspection, and provides shared access to common services like Active Directory. Which approach best achieves these goals?',
  'mcq',
  '["Establish a full mesh of Amazon VPC peering connections between all VPCs.","Deploy a separate security VPC and a shared services VPC, connected via AWS Transit Gateway.","Use AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections from each business unit''s VPC to the shared services VPC.","Configure AWS PrivateLink endpoints in each VPC to access shared services."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Deploy a separate security VPC and a shared services VPC, connected via AWS Transit Gateway) is the best approach. This architecture uses AWS Transit Gateway to connect all business unit VPCs (spoke VPCs) to a central hub. Traffic between spoke VPCs or to shared services can be routed through a dedicated security VPC for centralized inspection (e.g., with firewalls) and then to a shared services VPC for common resources. Option A (Establish a full mesh of Amazon VPC peering connections) is an anti-pattern for large-scale multi-VPC environments due to its non-transitive nature and high operational overhead. Option C (Use AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections) is typically for hybrid connectivity and would be overly complex and inefficient for inter-VPC communication. Option D (Configure AWS PrivateLink endpoints) is for privately consuming specific services, not for general network segmentation, centralized inspection, or shared services VPC routing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_1',
  'medium'
);

