INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_35',
  'Which two AWS services provide centralized identity management and single sign-on (SSO) capabilities across multiple AWS accounts and business applications? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS IAM Identity Center (SSO)","AWS Organizations","AWS Directory Service","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","AWS Systems Manager"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (AWS IAM Identity Center (SSO)) provides a single sign-on experience to multiple AWS accounts and cloud applications, allowing users to sign in once to access all their assigned accounts and applications. Option C (AWS Directory Service) allows you to set up and run Microsoft Active Directory (AD) on AWS or connect your AWS resources to an existing on-premises AD. This can then be integrated with IAM Identity Center to provide centralized identity management. Option B (AWS Organizations) helps manage multiple accounts but is not an identity provider itself. Option D (AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)) is for sharing resources, not identity management. Option E (AWS Systems Manager) is for operational management, not identity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  102,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_36',
  'A company needs to ensure that all data stored at rest in Amazon S3 and Amazon RDS is encrypted. Which two services are fundamental for managing the encryption keys used for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (KMS)","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS CloudHSM","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (AWS Key Management Service (KMS)) is a managed service that makes it easy for you to create and control the encryption keys used to encrypt your data. Both Amazon S3 and Amazon RDS can integrate with KMS for server-side encryption with customer master keys (CMKs). Option D (AWS CloudHSM) provides hardware security modules (HSMs) in the AWS Cloud, allowing you to generate and use your own encryption keys on FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated HSMs. This offers a higher level of control for keys and can be used for S3 and RDS encryption. Option B (AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)) manages SSL/TLS certificates, not encryption keys for data at rest. Option C (AWS Secrets Manager) helps manage secrets like database credentials, not encryption keys. Option E (AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)) manages permissions to access keys but does not manage the keys themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  103,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_37',
  'A security team needs to monitor API activity across all AWS accounts in their organization and receive notifications for specific security-related events. Which two AWS services are essential for this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon EventBridge","AWS Security Hub","AWS Config","Amazon GuardDuty"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and related events made by users, roles, or AWS services in your AWS account, providing an audit trail of activity. This is fundamental for monitoring API activity. Option B (Amazon EventBridge) can be configured to receive events from CloudTrail (via CloudWatch Events) and route them to targets like Amazon SNS for notifications, AWS Lambda for custom processing, or AWS Security Hub for centralized security findings. This is essential for receiving notifications for specific events. Option C (AWS Security Hub) aggregates security findings but doesn''t directly monitor API activity or send custom notifications based on specific events. Option D (AWS Config) assesses and audits resource configurations, not API activity. Option E (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service, not primarily for general API activity monitoring and custom notifications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  104,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_38',
  'A company requires all data at rest in Amazon S3 buckets to be encrypted by default and wants to ensure that only authorized services and users can access the encryption keys. Which two AWS services are central to implementing this security control? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (KMS)","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Key Management Service (KMS)) is essential for creating and managing the encryption keys used for S3 server-side encryption (SSE-KMS). It allows fine-grained control over who can use the keys and provides an audit trail of key usage. Option B (AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)) is crucial for defining and managing the permissions for users and AWS services to interact with KMS keys and S3 buckets, ensuring that only authorized entities can access and use the encryption keys and encrypted data. Option C (AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)) manages SSL/TLS certificates, not encryption keys. Option D (AWS CloudTrail) logs API calls, providing an audit trail, but does not implement the encryption or access control itself. Option E (AWS Secrets Manager) manages secrets like database credentials, not encryption keys for S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  105,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_39',
  'Which two AWS services are designed to help you centrally manage and deploy security configurations, such as AWS WAF rules and security groups, across multiple AWS accounts in an organization? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Firewall Manager","AWS Organizations","AWS Security Hub","AWS Config","AWS IAM Identity Center"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Firewall Manager) allows you to centrally configure and manage firewall rules across your accounts and applications in AWS Organizations, including AWS WAF, AWS Shield Advanced, and Amazon VPC security groups. Option B (AWS Organizations) is the foundational service that enables Firewall Manager to operate across multiple accounts by grouping them into a single organization. Option C (AWS Security Hub) provides a comprehensive view of your security state but doesn''t manage firewall rules. Option D (AWS Config) assesses compliance but doesn''t centrally deploy security configurations. Option E (AWS IAM Identity Center) is for identity management, not security configuration management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  106,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_40',
  'A security team needs to enforce a consistent set of AWS WAF rules and security group configurations across all accounts and VPCs within their AWS Organization. Which two AWS services can centralize and automate the deployment and management of these security controls? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Firewall Manager","AWS Organizations","AWS IAM Identity Center","AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM)","AWS Control Tower"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Firewall Manager) is designed to centralize the configuration and deployment of AWS WAF rules, AWS Shield Advanced protections, and security groups across multiple accounts and VPCs in an AWS Organization. Option B (AWS Organizations) is the foundational service that enables Firewall Manager to operate across multiple accounts by providing the organizational structure and allowing policy delegation. Option C (AWS IAM Identity Center) is for centralized single sign-on, not security configuration deployment. Option D (AWS Resource Access Manager) is for sharing resources, not for deploying security configurations. Option E (AWS Control Tower) sets up a landing zone with guardrails but doesn''t specifically centralize WAF or security group deployment in the same way Firewall Manager does.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  107,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_41',
  'Which two statements accurately describe the purpose of permission boundaries in AWS IAM? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["They define the maximum permissions that an identity-based policy can grant to an IAM entity.","They are used to grant permissions to users or roles within an account.","They can delegate administrative tasks to development teams while ensuring compliance.","They restrict the maximum permissions available to accounts within an Organizational Unit (OU).","They centralize the management of security groups across multiple accounts."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) states that permission boundaries define the maximum permissions that an identity-based policy can grant to an IAM entity (user or role), acting as an ''upper limit'' on what the entity can do. Option C (correct) highlights a key use case: they allow for the delegation of administrative tasks (like creating users or roles) to development teams, while ensuring that the created entities cannot exceed the permissions defined by the boundary, thus maintaining compliance. Option B (incorrect) describes the function of identity-based policies, not permission boundaries. Option D (incorrect) describes the function of Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations. Option E (incorrect) describes a function of AWS Firewall Manager.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  108,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'medium'
);

