INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_4_89',
  'A development team needs to rapidly provision new AWS accounts for project isolation, ensuring they adhere to baseline security policies. Order the steps for account vending within AWS Organizations.',
  'ordering',
  '["Grant initial access to the new account for administrators","Define the account creation process and baseline configurations","Create a new AWS account within AWS Organizations","Apply baseline Service Control Policies (SCPs) to the new account''s OU"]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'The process of account vending begins with defining the account creation process and baseline configurations, including security standards (Option B). Next, a new AWS account is created within AWS Organizations (Option C), typically placed into a specific Organizational Unit (OU). Baseline Service Control Policies (SCPs) are then applied to that OU, ensuring the new account adheres to organizational governance (Option D). Finally, initial administrative access is granted to the new account for setup and configuration (Option A).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_4_90',
  'A large enterprise is establishing a new multi-account AWS environment and wants to implement a well-architected landing zone with automated governance, security baselines, and account provisioning. They need to ensure consistent guardrails across all new accounts. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to set up AWS Control Tower.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure AWS Control Tower with the desired Organizational Units (OUs) and guardrails.","Set up a new AWS Organization or use an existing one.","Enroll existing AWS accounts into Control Tower or provision new accounts through Account Factory.","Review and customize the default Control Tower guardrails to meet specific compliance requirements."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'The foundational step for AWS Control Tower is to have an AWS Organization in place (Option B), as Control Tower operates within an Organization. Once the Organization is ready, AWS Control Tower is configured, which involves defining the initial OUs and selecting the guardrails to be enforced (Option A). After the initial setup, it''s crucial to review and customize these default guardrails to align with the enterprise''s specific security and compliance needs (Option D). Finally, existing accounts can be enrolled, or new accounts can be provisioned using the Account Factory, which automatically applies the defined guardrails (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_4_91',
  'An organization uses AWS Organizations to manage multiple accounts. They need to enforce a policy that prevents any account within a specific Organizational Unit (OU) from launching EC2 instances in unsupported Regions, while allowing other OUs more flexibility. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to implement this using Service Control Policies (SCPs).',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new Organizational Unit (OU) for the accounts requiring the restriction.","Create a Service Control Policy (SCP) that explicitly denies EC2 instance launches in specific Regions.","Move the relevant AWS accounts into the newly created OU.","Attach the SCP to the newly created OU."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'To apply a specific SCP to a subset of accounts, the first logical step is to create an Organizational Unit (OU) to group those accounts (Option A). Next, the Service Control Policy (SCP) itself is crafted with the explicit denial statement for EC2 instance launches in the unsupported Regions (Option B). Once the SCP is defined, it is attached to the newly created OU (Option D). Finally, the relevant AWS accounts are moved into this OU, at which point the SCP will take effect on them (Option C). This order ensures the policy is ready and attached before accounts are subjected to it.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_4_92',
  'A company with a growing number of AWS accounts wants to simplify user access management. They need to provide their employees with a single sign-on experience to access all authorized AWS accounts and applications, integrating with their existing corporate directory. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to set up IAM Identity Center (SSO).',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure IAM Identity Center to use the corporate directory as its identity source.","Enable IAM Identity Center in the AWS Organization''s management account.","Create permission sets in IAM Identity Center to define access levels for AWS accounts.","Assign users and groups from the corporate directory to specific AWS accounts with defined permission sets."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The initial step for centralizing access is to enable IAM Identity Center within the AWS Organization''s management account (Option B). Once enabled, IAM Identity Center needs to be configured to use the corporate directory (e.g., Active Directory, Okta) as its identity source (Option A). After the identity source is connected, permission sets are created within IAM Identity Center to define the specific access levels users will have in different AWS accounts (Option C). Finally, users and groups from the corporate directory are assigned to AWS accounts with these permission sets, granting them single sign-on access (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_4_93',
  'A development team in one AWS account has created a shared Transit Gateway that needs to be used by application VPCs in other AWS accounts within the same organization. They want to share this network resource securely and efficiently. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to share the AWS Transit Gateway using AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM).',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a resource share in AWS RAM, specifying the Transit Gateway as the resource.","Accept the resource share invitation in the recipient AWS accounts.","Specify the AWS accounts or Organizational Units (OUs) that can access the shared resource.","Create an AWS Transit Gateway in the owner AWS account."]'::json,
  '{3,0,2,1}',
  'The prerequisite for sharing any resource is that the resource must first exist. Therefore, creating the AWS Transit Gateway in the owner account is the initial step (Option D). Once the Transit Gateway is provisioned, a resource share is created in AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM), identifying the Transit Gateway as the resource to be shared (Option A). Within the resource share, the specific AWS accounts or OUs that are authorized to access the Transit Gateway are defined (Option C). Finally, the recipient AWS accounts must accept the resource share invitation to gain access to the shared Transit Gateway (Option B).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_4_94',
  'An organization wants to centralize security monitoring for all accounts using AWS Security Hub. To avoid giving the Security Hub administrator full permissions in the management account, they need to delegate the administration of Security Hub to a dedicated security account. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to delegate Security Hub administration in AWS Organizations.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable AWS Security Hub in the designated security account.","Designate the security account as the delegated administrator for Security Hub in AWS Organizations.","Enable AWS Security Hub in the AWS Organization''s management account.","Enable Security Hub in member accounts and configure auto-enablement for new accounts."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'The process of delegating Security Hub administration begins by enabling AWS Security Hub in the AWS Organization''s management account (Option C), which is a prerequisite for delegation. Next, the designated security account is explicitly set as the delegated administrator for Security Hub within AWS Organizations (Option B). After delegation, Security Hub is enabled in the security account (Option A), which will now manage Security Hub for the entire organization. Finally, Security Hub is enabled in member accounts, and auto-enablement is configured for any future accounts to ensure consistent coverage (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_4_95',
  'A company is rapidly expanding its AWS footprint and needs to quickly provision new AWS accounts for development teams. Each new account must adhere to the organization''s security and compliance baselines automatically. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to provision a new account using AWS Control Tower''s Account Factory.',
  'ordering',
  '["Log in to the AWS Control Tower management account.","Navigate to the Account Factory in the Control Tower console.","Specify the desired Organizational Unit (OU) for the new account and provide account details.","Initiate the provisioning process for the new AWS account."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step to provision a new account via Account Factory is to log into the AWS Control Tower management account (Option A), as this is where Control Tower is managed. From there, the user navigates to the Account Factory section within the Control Tower console (Option B). The details for the new account, including the target Organizational Unit (OU) and account name/email, are then specified (Option C). Finally, the provisioning process is initiated, which automates the creation of the new account with all defined guardrails and baselines (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_4_96',
  'An enterprise uses an external identity provider (IdP) for all employee authentication. They want to integrate this IdP with AWS to provide a seamless single sign-on experience for their users to access AWS accounts managed by AWS Organizations. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to integrate an external IdP with IAM Identity Center.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the external IdP to trust IAM Identity Center as a service provider.","Enable IAM Identity Center in the AWS Organization''s management account.","Configure IAM Identity Center to use an external identity source and provide IdP metadata.","Create permission sets and assign users/groups from the IdP to AWS accounts."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'The initial step is to enable IAM Identity Center in the AWS Organization''s management account (Option B). Once enabled, IAM Identity Center needs to be configured to use an external identity source, and the IdP metadata (e.g., SAML metadata) is provided to IAM Identity Center (Option C). Concurrently, the external IdP must be configured to trust IAM Identity Center as a service provider, exchanging necessary metadata (Option A). Finally, after the trust relationship is established, permission sets are created in IAM Identity Center, and users/groups from the external IdP are assigned to AWS accounts with these permission sets (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_4_97',
  'A central networking team manages a core VPC with specific subnet configurations that need to be used by application teams in different AWS accounts. The application teams should be able to launch resources into these pre-defined subnets without owning the VPC itself. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to share subnets across accounts using AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM).',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a resource share in AWS RAM, specifying the subnets to be shared.","Accept the resource share invitation in the recipient AWS accounts.","Specify the AWS accounts or Organizational Units (OUs) that can access the shared subnets.","Create a VPC and subnets in the central networking account."]'::json,
  '{3,0,2,1}',
  'The prerequisite for sharing subnets is that the VPC and subnets must first be created in the owner account (Option D). Once the subnets are provisioned, a resource share is created in AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM), identifying the specific subnets to be shared (Option A). Within the resource share, the specific AWS accounts or OUs that are authorized to access the subnets are defined (Option C). Finally, the recipient AWS accounts must accept the resource share invitation to gain access to the shared subnets, allowing them to launch resources into them (Option B).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_4_98',
  'An organization wants to enforce specific security guardrails across a new set of development accounts within its AWS Organization. Order the following steps to correctly apply a Service Control Policy (SCP) to an Organizational Unit (OU).',
  'ordering',
  '["Create the Service Control Policy (SCP) document with the desired permissions.","Attach the SCP to the target Organizational Unit (OU).","Test the SCP''s effect on an affected account.","Ensure AWS Organizations is enabled with all features."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'Before applying any SCPs, AWS Organizations must be enabled with all features (Option D) to allow SCP functionality. Next, you create the Service Control Policy (SCP) document itself, defining the permissions or restrictions (Option A). Once the SCP is defined, you attach it to the specific Organizational Unit (OU) that contains the accounts you want to govern (Option B). Finally, it is crucial to test the SCP''s effect on an affected account (Option C) to ensure it behaves as expected and doesn''t inadvertently block necessary actions.',
