  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_3_89',
  'A global e-commerce company needs a highly available and disaster-resilient database solution that can provide fast local reads and rapid recovery across multiple AWS Regions with minimal data loss. They are currently using an Amazon Aurora MySQL-compatible database. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to configure Amazon Aurora Global Database.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a secondary Aurora DB cluster in the desired secondary AWS Region.","Modify the primary Aurora DB cluster to enable Global Database.","Add the secondary Aurora DB cluster to the Global Database.","Configure application endpoints to use the appropriate regional Aurora cluster for reads."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To set up an Aurora Global Database, the existing primary Aurora DB cluster must first be modified to enable the Global Database feature (Option B). Next, a secondary Aurora DB cluster is created in the desired secondary AWS Region (Option A), which will serve as the replication target. This newly created secondary cluster is then added to the Global Database (Option C), initiating the cross-Region replication. Finally, application endpoints are configured to direct read traffic to the local Aurora cluster in each Region, optimizing for low-latency reads (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_3_90',
  'A gaming application requires a globally distributed, active-active NoSQL database that can serve users with low-latency reads and writes from any Region. The application must remain available even if an entire AWS Region becomes unavailable. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to configure Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a DynamoDB table in a primary AWS Region.","Enable DynamoDB Streams on the primary table.","Add additional AWS Regions to the DynamoDB table to create replicas.","Configure the application to perform reads and writes to the local replica in each Region."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The process begins by creating a DynamoDB table in a primary AWS Region (Option A), which will serve as the initial data source. Next, DynamoDB Streams must be enabled on this primary table (Option B), as Streams are fundamental for replicating data changes across Regions. After Streams are enabled, additional AWS Regions are added to the DynamoDB table, which automatically creates replicas and initiates global replication (Option C). Finally, the application is configured to interact with the local DynamoDB table replica in each Region, enabling active-active, low-latency reads and writes globally (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_3_91',
  'A web application is deployed in an active-passive configuration across two AWS Regions to improve resilience and reduce latency for global users. The company needs to direct user traffic to the optimal healthy endpoint, automatically failing over if the primary Region experiences issues. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to implement this using AWS Global Accelerator.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator.","Add endpoint groups for each AWS Region where the application is deployed.","Add the application''s Elastic IP addresses or Network Load Balancers as endpoints within each regional endpoint group.","Update DNS records to point to the Global Accelerator''s static IP addresses."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step is to create an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator (Option A), which provides the static IP addresses that users will connect to. Next, endpoint groups are added for each AWS Region where the application is deployed (Option B), allowing Global Accelerator to manage traffic distribution across these regions. Within each regional endpoint group, the application''s specific endpoints, such as Elastic IP addresses or Network Load Balancers, are added (Option C). Finally, DNS records are updated to point to the Global Accelerator''s static IP addresses, directing user traffic through the accelerator for optimal routing and failover (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_3_92',
  'A reliability engineering team wants to proactively test the resilience of their production application by injecting controlled faults, such as terminating EC2 instances or network latency, to understand how the system behaves under stress and identify weaknesses. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to conduct a chaos engineering experiment using AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS).',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the experiment template in AWS FIS, specifying target resources and actions (e.g., terminate EC2 instances).","Identify the specific application or service components to be targeted for fault injection.","Run the FIS experiment and monitor the application''s behavior and metrics.","Define experiment stop conditions to automatically halt the experiment if critical thresholds are breached."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'Before injecting any faults, it''s crucial to identify the specific application or service components that will be targeted (Option B) to ensure the experiment is focused. Once targets are known, the experiment template is defined in AWS FIS, outlining the fault injection actions (e.g., terminating EC2 instances) and the specific resources to be affected (Option A). To ensure safety, experiment stop conditions are defined to automatically halt the experiment if any critical metrics or thresholds are breached (Option D). Finally, the FIS experiment is run, and the application''s behavior and relevant metrics are closely monitored to observe the impact of the injected faults (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_3_93',
  'A financial services application needs to be highly available and performant for users worldwide. It is deployed in an active-active configuration across multiple AWS Regions, and the company wants to ensure optimal routing of user requests to the nearest healthy application endpoint. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to implement this architecture using AWS Global Accelerator.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy the application in an active-active configuration across multiple AWS Regions.","Create an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator.","Add regional endpoint groups to the accelerator, each containing the application''s load balancers or Elastic IPs.","Update DNS records to point to the Global Accelerator''s static IP addresses."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The foundational step for an active-active multi-Region application is to deploy the application itself in each desired AWS Region (Option A). Once the application is running, an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator is created to provide the static entry points for global traffic (Option B). Next, regional endpoint groups are added to the accelerator, and the application''s load balancers or Elastic IPs in each Region are registered as endpoints within their respective groups (Option C). Finally, DNS records are updated to point to the Global Accelerator''s static IP addresses, directing global user traffic through the accelerator for optimal routing and failover (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_3_94',
  'A company has deployed an Amazon Aurora Global Database for disaster recovery. They need to regularly test their recovery procedures to ensure that a planned or unplanned failover to the secondary Region can be executed successfully with minimal downtime and data loss. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to perform a planned failover of an Aurora Global Database.',
  'ordering',
  '["Verify the replication lag between the primary and secondary clusters is minimal.","Promote the secondary Aurora DB cluster in the target Region to be the new primary.","Stop writes to the original primary Aurora DB cluster.","Update application connection strings to point to the new primary cluster."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'Before initiating any failover, it is critical to stop writes to the original primary Aurora DB cluster (Option C) to prevent new data from being written that might not replicate. Then, verify that the replication lag between the primary and secondary clusters is minimal (Option A) to ensure the lowest possible RPO (Recovery Point Objective), addressing the anti-pattern of not accounting for data replication lag. Once replication lag is acceptable, the secondary Aurora DB cluster in the target Region is promoted to become the new primary (Option B). Finally, application connection strings are updated to point to the newly promoted primary cluster, completing the failover (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_3_95',
  'A globally distributed application uses Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables for its data store. The development team needs to understand how DynamoDB handles concurrent writes to the same item from different Regions to ensure data consistency and predictable behavior. Arrange the following steps in the correct order that DynamoDB Global Tables uses for conflict resolution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Each replica independently processes the write and propagates it to other replicas via DynamoDB Streams.","The item''s attributes are updated based on the ''last writer wins'' conflict resolution strategy.","A client application writes an item to a local DynamoDB table replica in a specific AWS Region.","All replicas eventually converge to the same state for the item."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'The process begins when a client application writes an item to its local DynamoDB table replica in a specific AWS Region (Option C). This write is then independently processed by that replica and propagated to all other replicas in the Global Table via DynamoDB Streams (Option A). If concurrent writes to the same item occur from different Regions, DynamoDB Global Tables resolves these conflicts using a ''last writer wins'' strategy, where the write with the latest server-side timestamp prevails (Option B). As a result of this conflict resolution and replication, all replicas eventually converge to the same consistent state for the item (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_3_96',
  'A global e-commerce company requires a highly available and resilient relational database with fast cross-Region disaster recovery capabilities. Order the following steps to correctly set up an Amazon Aurora Global Database.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a secondary Aurora DB cluster in a different AWS Region.","Promote the secondary cluster to become the new primary in a disaster recovery scenario.","Create an Amazon Aurora DB cluster in your primary AWS Region.","Add the secondary cluster to the global database configuration."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'To set up an Amazon Aurora Global Database, you first create an Amazon Aurora DB cluster in your primary AWS Region (Option C). Next, you create a secondary Aurora DB cluster in a different AWS Region (Option A) that will serve as the replication target. After both clusters are created, you add the secondary cluster to the global database configuration (Option D), establishing the replication link. In a disaster recovery scenario, the final step would be to promote the secondary cluster to become the new primary (Option B) to restore full write capabilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_3_97',
  'A development team needs to extend an existing Amazon DynamoDB table to support multi-Region active-active replication for improved availability and local data access. Order the following steps to correctly enable DynamoDB global tables.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the existing DynamoDB table in the AWS Management Console.","Add additional AWS Regions to the global table configuration.","Ensure the existing table has DynamoDB Streams enabled with the ''New and old images'' view.","Confirm the global table replication is active across all specified Regions."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'To enable DynamoDB global tables for an existing table, you first select the existing DynamoDB table in the AWS Management Console (Option A). It is crucial to ensure the existing table has DynamoDB Streams enabled with the ''New and old images'' view (Option C), as this is a prerequisite for global tables to capture changes. Then, you add additional AWS Regions to the global table configuration (Option B), specifying where the replicas should be created. Finally, you confirm that the global table replication is active across all specified Regions (Option D) to ensure data consistency and availability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_3',
  'medium'
);

