  'm_42_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_44',
  'A global enterprise wants to protect all its internet-facing resources, including Elastic Load Balancers and Elastic IP addresses, against DDoS attacks using AWS Shield Advanced. They need a way to centrally enable and manage Shield Advanced protection across multiple accounts and Regions. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to achieve this using AWS Firewall Manager.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Firewall Manager policy for AWS Shield Advanced, specifying the protection scope.","Designate a security account as the AWS Firewall Manager administrator in AWS Organizations.","Subscribe the Firewall Manager administrator account to AWS Shield Advanced.","Associate the Firewall Manager policy with the Organizational Units (OUs) or specific accounts containing the internet-facing resources."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'To centrally manage Shield Advanced, the first step is to designate a security account as the AWS Firewall Manager administrator within AWS Organizations (Option B). This account will centrally manage policies. Next, this administrator account must subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced (Option C), as Shield Advanced is a paid service. A Firewall Manager policy for AWS Shield Advanced is then created, defining the scope of resources to be protected (Option A). Finally, this Firewall Manager policy is associated with the target Organizational Units (OUs) or specific accounts, causing Firewall Manager to automatically apply Shield Advanced protection to all specified internet-facing resources (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_45',
  'After configuring IAM Identity Center and connecting it to their corporate directory, an administrator needs to grant specific users or groups access to an AWS account with a predefined set of permissions. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to assign access to an AWS account using IAM Identity Center.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the AWS account to which access will be granted.","Choose the permission set that defines the access level.","Select the users or groups from the corporate directory to be granted access.","Review and confirm the assignment to create the access configuration."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'To assign access, the administrator first selects the specific AWS account to which access will be granted (Option A). Next, the users or groups from the corporate directory who require access to this account are selected (Option C). After identifying the users/groups, the appropriate permission set, which defines the level of access, is chosen (Option B). Finally, the assignment is reviewed and confirmed, which provisions the necessary roles in the target AWS account and enables SSO access for the selected users/groups (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_46',
  'A new enterprise is starting its journey on AWS and needs to establish a foundational multi-account structure that supports governance, security, and cost management from day one. They plan to use AWS Organizations to manage their accounts. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to set up AWS Organizations for a new enterprise.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable all features in AWS Organizations.","Create an AWS Organization in the management account.","Create Organizational Units (OUs) to group accounts based on function or compliance needs.","Invite existing AWS accounts or create new accounts within the Organization."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The foundational step is to create an AWS Organization in the designated management account (Option B). Once the Organization is created, it''s crucial to enable all features (Option A) to unlock advanced capabilities like Service Control Policies (SCPs) and delegated administration. Next, Organizational Units (OUs) are created to establish a logical hierarchy for grouping accounts, which is essential for applying granular policies and managing governance (Option C). Finally, existing AWS accounts are invited to join the Organization, or new accounts are created directly within the Organization (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_47',
  'A central networking team has provisioned an AWS Transit Gateway in a dedicated network account. They need to share this Transit Gateway with multiple application VPCs residing in different AWS accounts within the same AWS Organization to enable inter-VPC connectivity. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to share the Transit Gateway using AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM).',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a resource share in AWS RAM, specifying the Transit Gateway as the resource to share.","Create an AWS Transit Gateway in the dedicated network account.","Specify the AWS accounts or Organizational Units (OUs) that will receive access to the shared Transit Gateway.","Accept the resource share invitation in each recipient AWS account (if auto-accept is not configured)."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The initial step is to create the AWS Transit Gateway in the dedicated network account (Option B), as it''s the resource to be shared. Once the Transit Gateway exists, a resource share is created in AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM), identifying the Transit Gateway as the resource to be shared (Option A). Within the resource share, the specific AWS accounts or OUs that are authorized to access the Transit Gateway are defined (Option C). Finally, each recipient AWS account must accept the resource share invitation to gain access to the shared Transit Gateway (Option D), unless auto-accept is configured for the Organization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_48',
  'A large organization needs to centralize identity management and provide single sign-on access to multiple AWS accounts and business applications. Order the following steps to correctly configure AWS IAM Identity Center (SSO).',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable AWS IAM Identity Center in the AWS Organizations management account.","Assign users and groups to permission sets for specific AWS accounts.","Connect an external identity source (e.g., Active Directory or Okta) or use IAM Identity Center''s own identity store.","Create permission sets that define the access levels for users in target AWS accounts."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}',
  'To configure AWS IAM Identity Center, you must first enable it in the AWS Organizations management account (Option A). Once enabled, you connect an external identity source, such as Active Directory or Okta, or utilize IAM Identity Center''s built-in identity store to manage users and groups (Option C). Next, you create permission sets (Option D), which are collections of administrative permissions that define the access levels users will have in the target AWS accounts. Finally, you assign users and groups to these permission sets for specific AWS accounts (Option B), granting them the defined access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_42_m_42_2_49',
  'A security team wants to centrally deploy and manage a web application firewall (WAF) across multiple accounts and applications within their AWS Organization. Order the following steps to correctly use AWS Firewall Manager for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new AWS Firewall Manager policy and specify the WAF rule groups or rules to apply.","Delegate an AWS account as the Firewall Manager administrator.","Define the scope of the policy, including target accounts, resource types, and Regions.","Monitor the policy''s compliance status and deployed resources in the Firewall Manager console."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To use AWS Firewall Manager, the initial step is to delegate an AWS account as the Firewall Manager administrator (Option B) within AWS Organizations. This account will manage all Firewall Manager policies. Next, you create a new AWS Firewall Manager policy (Option A) and specify the WAF rule groups or rules that you want to apply. After defining the WAF rules, you define the scope of the policy (Option C), including which target accounts, resource types (e.g., Application Load Balancers, Amazon CloudFront distributions), and AWS Regions the policy should apply to. Finally, you monitor the policy''s compliance status and the resources it has deployed or remediated in the Firewall Manager console (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain42_9',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_42_2',
  'medium'
);

