INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_41',
  'A data analytics startup processes petabytes of customer data monthly, storing it in Amazon S3. The data is accessed frequently for the first 30 days for real-time dashboards, then infrequently for another 60 days for historical reporting, and finally archived for compliance for several years. The company is experiencing high AWS bills, particularly from data storage and data transfer costs. A Solutions Architect needs to design a cost optimization strategy that aligns storage costs with access patterns and minimizes data transfer charges, without compromising data availability for required access tiers. Which two strategies should be implemented? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implement Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies to transition data from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days, then to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 90 days.","Utilize Amazon CloudFront with caching for frequently accessed dashboard data to reduce S3 direct access and egress costs.","Store all data in Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval from ingestion to minimize storage costs immediately.","Use Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration for all data uploads and downloads to reduce data transfer costs, regardless of user location.","Deploy a dedicated Amazon EC2 instance with a large local SSD to cache frequently accessed data instead of using S3."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) directly addresses storage cost optimization by implementing Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies. This strategy correctly aligns data storage tiers with access patterns: S3 Standard for frequent access (first 30 days), S3 Standard-IA for infrequent access (next 60 days), and S3 Glacier Deep Archive for long-term archival. This significantly reduces storage costs over time. Option B (correct) addresses data transfer cost optimization by utilizing Amazon CloudFront. By caching frequently accessed dashboard data at edge locations, CloudFront reduces direct S3 access requests and minimizes data egress costs from the S3 bucket, especially for global users, aligning with the anti-pattern of not addressing data transfer costs (ap-2.4-001). Option C (incorrect) suggests storing all data in S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval from ingestion. While S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is very cost-effective for long-term archival, it has higher retrieval costs and longer retrieval times, making it unsuitable for data accessed frequently for real-time dashboards during the first 30 days. Option D (incorrect) proposes using Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration for all data transfers. While Transfer Acceleration can speed up uploads/downloads over long distances, it incurs additional costs and is not primarily a cost-optimization strategy for general data transfer, especially if users are geographically close to the S3 bucket. Option E (incorrect) suggests deploying a dedicated EC2 instance for caching. This introduces significant operational overhead for managing the instance, storage, and cache invalidation, and it would likely be more expensive and less scalable than leveraging managed services like CloudFront or ElastiCache for caching, failing the goal of cost optimization through managed services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_42',
  'A large enterprise is migrating its on-premises data warehouse to AWS. The data warehouse contains petabytes of historical sales data, which is accessed frequently by business intelligence tools for the first 90 days, then infrequently for quarterly reports, and finally needs to be retained for 7 years for compliance. The Solutions Architect needs to design a storage solution that is highly available, durable, cost-optimized, and automatically transitions data between storage classes based on access patterns. Which two AWS services or features should be recommended? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Store the data in Amazon S3 and implement S3 Lifecycle policies to transition data through S3 Standard, S3 Standard-IA, and S3 Glacier Deep Archive.","Utilize Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering to automatically move data between access tiers based on changing access patterns.","Deploy Amazon FSx for Lustre for high-performance file storage, and manually move older data to S3 for archival.","Provision a large Amazon EBS gp3 volume attached to an EC2 instance to store all historical data.","Use Amazon S3 One Zone-IA for all data storage to achieve the lowest possible storage cost."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a highly effective strategy for cost-optimizing storage in Amazon S3. By implementing S3 Lifecycle policies, data can be automatically transitioned from S3 Standard (frequently accessed) to S3 Standard-IA (infrequently accessed) after 90 days, and then to S3 Glacier Deep Archive (long-term archival) after a specified period (e.g., 7 years minus 90 days), aligning storage costs with access patterns and compliance requirements. Option B (correct) offers another excellent cost optimization strategy for S3. Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between two access tiers (frequent and infrequent) when access patterns change, without performance impact. This is particularly useful when access patterns are unknown or changing, ensuring data is always in the most cost-effective tier while maintaining high availability and durability. Option C (incorrect) suggests Amazon FSx for Lustre. While FSx for Lustre provides high-performance file storage, it is primarily designed for high-performance computing workloads and is not a cost-effective solution for petabytes of historical data that will eventually be archived. Manual movement to S3 also increases operational overhead. Option D (incorrect) proposes provisioning a large Amazon EBS gp3 volume. EBS volumes are block storage attached to EC2 instances and are not suitable for petabyte-scale, highly available, and cost-optimized archival storage. Managing such a large volume and its backups would be complex and expensive. Option E (incorrect) recommends Amazon S3 One Zone-IA. While cost-effective, S3 One Zone-IA stores data in a single Availability Zone, meaning data could be lost in an AZ outage. This violates the requirement for high availability and durability for mission-critical historical sales data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_43',
  'A media company needs to process large video files (up to 100 GB each) for transcoding and content analysis. The processing jobs are compute-intensive and can take several hours to complete. The company wants to optimize costs by leveraging spare compute capacity, but the jobs are fault-tolerant and can be interrupted and resumed. The Solutions Architect needs to design a cost-optimized compute solution that can handle these batch processing workloads efficiently. Which two AWS services or features should be recommended? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilize Amazon EC2 Spot Instances within an Auto Scaling group for the compute-intensive transcoding and analysis tasks.","Implement AWS Batch to orchestrate and manage the execution of containerized video processing jobs on Spot Instances.","Provision dedicated On-Demand Amazon EC2 instances with the largest available instance types to ensure job completion.","Use AWS Lambda functions with increased memory and timeout settings for processing the large video files.","Purchase Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for the batch processing workloads to guarantee capacity and reduce costs."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends utilizing Amazon EC2 Spot Instances. Spot Instances are ideal for fault-tolerant, flexible, and compute-intensive batch workloads like video transcoding and analysis because they allow users to bid for unused EC2 capacity at significantly reduced prices. Their interruptible nature is acceptable since the jobs can be resumed, directly addressing the cost optimization goal. Option B (correct) suggests implementing AWS Batch. AWS Batch is a fully managed service that enables developers, scientists, and engineers to easily run hundreds of thousands of batch computing jobs on AWS. It can automatically provision and manage compute resources, including Spot Instances, and orchestrate containerized workloads, making it perfect for managing the execution of the video processing jobs efficiently and cost-effectively. Option C (incorrect) proposes provisioning dedicated On-Demand EC2 instances. While this guarantees capacity, On-Demand instances are significantly more expensive than Spot Instances, failing the primary constraint of cost optimization for fault-tolerant batch workloads. Option D (incorrect) suggests using AWS Lambda functions. While Lambda is serverless, its current limitations (e.g., maximum execution duration, memory limits, payload size) make it unsuitable for processing very large video files (up to 100 GB) and long-running, compute-intensive tasks that can take several hours. Option E (incorrect) recommends purchasing Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances. Reserved Instances are suitable for steady-state, predictable workloads where capacity is needed continuously. Batch processing with interruptible jobs, however, is better suited for Spot Instances for maximum cost savings, as Reserved Instances would lock in costs for potentially idle capacity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_44',
  'A large enterprise is building a new data analytics platform on AWS. The platform needs to process petabytes of data from various sources, requiring high-performance compute and storage. The data processing jobs are highly parallelizable and can tolerate interruptions. The Solutions Architect needs to design a cost-optimized solution for the compute layer that can scale massively for these batch processing workloads. Which two AWS services or features should be recommended? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilize Amazon EC2 Spot Instances within an Auto Scaling group for the data processing compute cluster.","Implement AWS Batch to orchestrate and manage the execution of containerized data processing jobs on Spot Instances.","Provision dedicated On-Demand Amazon EC2 instances with the largest available instance types for all processing.","Use AWS Lambda functions with increased memory and timeout settings for processing petabytes of data.","Purchase Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for the data processing workloads to guarantee capacity and reduce costs."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends utilizing Amazon EC2 Spot Instances. Spot Instances are ideal for highly parallelizable, fault-tolerant batch workloads like large-scale data processing because they allow users to bid for unused EC2 capacity at significantly reduced prices. Their interruptible nature is acceptable since the jobs can tolerate interruptions, directly addressing the cost optimization goal for massive scale. Option B (correct) suggests implementing AWS Batch. AWS Batch is a fully managed service that enables developers, scientists, and engineers to easily run hundreds of thousands of batch computing jobs on AWS. It can automatically provision and manage compute resources, including Spot Instances, and orchestrate containerized workloads, making it perfect for managing the execution of large-scale data processing jobs efficiently and cost-effectively. Option C (incorrect) proposes provisioning dedicated On-Demand EC2 instances. While this guarantees capacity, On-Demand instances are significantly more expensive than Spot Instances, failing the primary constraint of cost optimization for fault-tolerant batch workloads at massive scale. Option D (incorrect) suggests using AWS Lambda functions. While Lambda is serverless, its current limitations (e.g., maximum execution duration, memory limits, payload size) make it unsuitable for processing petabytes of data in long-running, compute-intensive tasks that are typically handled by batch processing frameworks. Option E (incorrect) recommends purchasing Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances. Reserved Instances are suitable for steady-state, predictable workloads where capacity is needed continuously. Batch processing with interruptible jobs, however, is better suited for Spot Instances for maximum cost savings, as Reserved Instances would lock in costs for potentially idle capacity that might not always be utilized.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_45',
  'A solutions architect is tasked with reducing the operational costs of a data-intensive application. The application frequently transfers large volumes of data between AWS Regions and to the internet. Which strategies can help optimize data transfer costs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use Amazon CloudFront for content delivery","Utilize VPC endpoints for inter-service communication","Migrate all data to a single Availability Zone","Increase EC2 instance sizes","Use Amazon S3 Standard-IA for all data"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Use Amazon CloudFront for content delivery) helps optimize data transfer costs by caching content at edge locations, reducing egress traffic from origin servers to the internet and between regions. Option B (Utilize VPC endpoints for inter-service communication) allows private connections to AWS services from within a VPC, avoiding data transfer over the internet or through NAT gateways, which can incur higher costs. Option C (Migrate all data to a single Availability Zone) does not inherently reduce data transfer costs, and could reduce reliability. Option D (Increase EC2 instance sizes) would likely increase compute costs, not reduce data transfer costs. Option E (Use Amazon S3 Standard-IA for all data) is a storage tier optimization, not a data transfer cost optimization strategy, and might not be suitable for frequently accessed data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_46',
  'A company needs to store large amounts of infrequently accessed data in Amazon S3 while minimizing storage costs. They also want to ensure that data that is rarely accessed is automatically moved to a lower-cost storage tier after a certain period. Which S3 features and lifecycle policies can help achieve this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["S3 Intelligent-Tiering","S3 Lifecycle policies","S3 Transfer Acceleration","S3 Multi-Region Access Points","S3 Object Lock"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (S3 Intelligent-Tiering) automatically moves data between two access tiers (frequently and infrequently accessed) based on access patterns, optimizing storage costs without performance impact or operational overhead. Option B (S3 Lifecycle policies) allow defining rules to automatically transition objects to different S3 storage classes (e.g., S3 Standard-IA, S3 Glacier) or expire them after a specified time, which is crucial for storage cost optimization. Option C (S3 Transfer Acceleration) speeds up large-distance data transfers to S3, but it''s not a storage cost optimization feature. Option D (S3 Multi-Region Access Points) provides a single global endpoint for S3 buckets across multiple regions, but it''s not a storage cost optimization feature. Option E (S3 Object Lock) provides WORM (Write Once Read Many) capabilities for data retention, not cost optimization through tiering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_47',
  'A solutions architect is designing a new serverless application and needs to evaluate the cost implications compared to a traditional provisioned compute model. They want to identify the key cost advantages of serverless. Which aspects are typically more cost-effective in a serverless model? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Paying only for actual compute duration","Elimination of idle compute costs","Fixed monthly infrastructure costs","Predictable scaling costs","Reduced data transfer costs"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Paying only for actual compute duration) is a fundamental advantage of serverless models like AWS Lambda, where you are billed based on the number of requests and the duration your code runs, rather than for always-on servers. Option B (Elimination of idle compute costs) is a direct consequence of paying only for active usage; when a serverless function is not invoked, it incurs no compute cost, unlike provisioned servers that accrue costs even when idle. Option C (Fixed monthly infrastructure costs) is characteristic of provisioned compute (e.g., EC2 Reserved Instances), not serverless. Option D (Predictable scaling costs) can be less predictable in serverless models for highly variable workloads, as costs directly correlate with usage. Option E (Reduced data transfer costs) is not an inherent advantage of serverless compute itself, as data transfer costs depend on architectural design regardless of compute model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_48',
  'A company has a consistent baseline workload for its Amazon ECS services but also experiences occasional unpredictable spikes in demand. They want to optimize costs for the predictable baseline while maintaining elasticity for spikes. Which AWS cost optimization strategies are suitable for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Savings Plans for committed usage","EC2 Spot Instances for baseline workload","Amazon EC2 On-Demand for baseline workload","S3 Intelligent-Tiering for compute","AWS CloudHSM for cost reduction"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Savings Plans for committed usage) allow for significant discounts on compute usage (including Fargate for ECS) in exchange for a 1- or 3-year commitment, ideal for the consistent baseline workload. Option B (EC2 Spot Instances for baseline workload) can be used for the fault-tolerant, stateless parts of the baseline workload or for the unpredictable spikes if the application can tolerate interruptions, offering substantial cost savings. Option C (Amazon EC2 On-Demand for baseline workload) is the most expensive option and does not provide cost optimization. Option D (S3 Intelligent-Tiering for compute) is a storage cost optimization feature, not applicable to compute. Option E (AWS CloudHSM for cost reduction) is a hardware security module and does not provide compute cost optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_49',
  'A solutions architect needs to design a cost-optimized storage solution for a large volume of historical log data that is accessed infrequently but must be retained for compliance for several years. The data needs to be highly durable. Which Amazon S3 storage classes are most appropriate for this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive","Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA)","Amazon S3 Standard","Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA)","Amazon FSx for Lustre"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive) is the lowest-cost S3 storage class for archiving data that is accessed rarely (once or twice a year) and can tolerate retrieval times of hours, making it ideal for long-term compliance data. Option B (Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA)) is suitable for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. It offers lower storage costs than S3 Standard but higher retrieval costs. Option C (Amazon S3 Standard) is for frequently accessed data and is more expensive than IA or Glacier tiers. Option D (Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA)) is lower cost than S3 Standard-IA but stores data in a single Availability Zone, making it less durable and not suitable for critical compliance data that needs high durability. Option E (Amazon FSx for Lustre) is a high-performance file system, not a cost-optimized archiving solution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_50',
  'A company is analyzing its AWS bill and identifies high data transfer costs, particularly for data moving between different AWS Regions and out to the internet. Which architectural adjustments can help reduce these data transfer costs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implement Amazon CloudFront for content delivery","Utilize VPC endpoints for private service access","Increase the number of EC2 instances","Store all data in S3 Standard storage class","Use AWS Lambda for all compute"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Implement Amazon CloudFront for content delivery) caches content at edge locations, reducing the amount of data transferred from origin servers to end-users over the internet, thereby lowering egress costs. Option B (Utilize VPC endpoints for private service access) allows services within a VPC to communicate with other AWS services (like S3 or DynamoDB) without traversing the public internet or requiring a NAT Gateway, which can significantly reduce data transfer costs. Option C (Increase the number of EC2 instances) would likely increase compute costs and potentially data transfer if traffic patterns are not optimized. Option D (Store all data in S3 Standard storage class) is a storage tier decision, not directly a data transfer cost optimization. Option E (Use AWS Lambda for all compute) is a compute model choice and does not inherently reduce data transfer costs, which depend on data movement patterns.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  98,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_51',
  'To reduce the cost of Amazon EC2 instances for committed, predictable workloads and for fault-tolerant, stateless batch jobs, which strategies should a solutions architect consider? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilizing Savings Plans","Leveraging EC2 Spot Instances","Running all workloads on On-Demand Instances","Increasing the instance size for all workloads","Migrating all workloads to AWS Lambda"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Utilizing Savings Plans) offers significant discounts (up to 72%) on compute usage in exchange for a commitment to a consistent amount of compute usage (measured in $/hour) for a 1-year or 3-year term, ideal for predictable workloads. Option B (Leveraging EC2 Spot Instances) allows you to bid for unused EC2 capacity, providing up to 90% savings compared to On-Demand prices, making them highly cost-effective for fault-tolerant, stateless, or batch workloads that can tolerate interruptions. Option C (Running all workloads on On-Demand Instances) is the most expensive option and does not provide cost savings. Option D (Increasing the instance size for all workloads) can increase costs if not justified by performance needs. Option E (Migrating all workloads to AWS Lambda) is a different architectural approach; while serverless can be cost-effective, it''s not a direct optimization for existing EC2 workloads and may not be suitable for all types of batch jobs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  99,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_52',
  'A company stores large amounts of data in Amazon S3, with varying access patterns. Which strategies can be used to optimize storage costs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implementing S3 Lifecycle policies for tiering","Utilizing Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering","Using Amazon Glacier Deep Archive for frequently accessed data","Disabling versioning on all S3 buckets","Storing all data in S3 Standard"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Implementing S3 Lifecycle policies for tiering) allows you to automatically transition objects between different S3 storage classes (e.g., from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA, then to S3 Glacier) based on their age or access patterns, optimizing costs. Option B (Utilizing Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering) automatically moves objects between two access tiers (frequent and infrequent access) based on changing access patterns, without performance impact or operational overhead, providing cost savings. Option C (Using Amazon Glacier Deep Archive for frequently accessed data) is incorrect, as Glacier Deep Archive is for archival data with retrieval times in hours, making it unsuitable for frequently accessed data. Option D (Disabling versioning on all S3 buckets) can reduce storage costs by preventing multiple versions of objects, but it also removes a critical data protection feature, which is generally not recommended for cost optimization. Option E (Storing all data in S3 Standard) is not cost-optimized for data with infrequent or archival access patterns, as S3 Standard is designed for frequently accessed data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  100,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_53',
  'To minimize data transfer costs for an application serving global users and accessing data from Amazon S3, which AWS services should be considered? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudFront","VPC endpoints","Amazon EC2 Spot Instances","AWS KMS","Amazon ElastiCache"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network (CDN) that caches content at edge locations closer to users, significantly reducing data transfer costs by serving content from the nearest edge location instead of the origin S3 bucket. Option B (VPC endpoints) allow private connections from your VPC to supported AWS services (like S3) without traversing the internet, which can reduce data transfer costs by keeping traffic within the AWS network. Option C (Amazon EC2 Spot Instances) are for compute cost optimization, not data transfer. Option D (AWS KMS) is for encryption key management. Option E (Amazon ElastiCache) is a caching service for databases or application data, not primarily for optimizing data transfer costs from S3 to global users.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  101,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_54',
  'When designing a cost-optimized solution, which factors are key considerations when comparing the cost models of serverless compute (like AWS Lambda) versus provisioned compute (like Amazon EC2)? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Idle time charges for provisioned compute","Per-request and duration charges for serverless compute","Operating system licensing costs for serverless","Fixed monthly costs for serverless","Data transfer costs between serverless functions"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Idle time charges for provisioned compute) is a critical factor, as you pay for Amazon EC2 instances even when they are not actively processing requests, leading to wasted costs during low usage periods. Option B (Per-request and duration charges for serverless compute) is the primary cost driver for AWS Lambda, where you only pay for the compute time consumed and the number of requests, making it cost-effective for intermittent or variable workloads. Option C (Operating system licensing costs for serverless) is not applicable to serverless services like Lambda, as AWS manages the underlying infrastructure and OS. Option D (Fixed monthly costs for serverless) is incorrect; serverless compute is billed on a pay-per-use model, not fixed monthly. Option E (Data transfer costs between serverless functions) can be a factor but is generally less significant than the core compute cost models for most applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  102,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_6_55',
  'Which AWS services can be used to monitor AWS spending, set budgets, and gain insights into cost optimization opportunities? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Cost Explorer","AWS Budgets","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Systems Manager","AWS CloudTrail"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Cost Explorer) allows you to visualize, understand, and manage your AWS costs and usage over time, providing detailed insights and forecasting. Option B (AWS Budgets) enables you to set custom budgets to track your costs and usage, and receive alerts when actual or forecasted costs exceed your budgeted amounts. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch) is a monitoring service for resources and applications, providing metrics and logs, but its primary focus is operational performance, not cost management. Option D (AWS Systems Manager) helps you manage your AWS resources and applications, but it is not a cost management tool. Option E (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and related events in your AWS account, primarily for auditing and security, not cost optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  103,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_6',
  'medium'
);

