INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_44_m_44_2_66',
  'A large organization operates multiple AWS accounts across several regions. Security teams currently monitor security findings from various AWS services (e.g., Amazon GuardDuty, AWS Config, Amazon Inspector) independently in each account and region. This distributed approach makes it difficult to get a consolidated view of the overall security posture and prioritize critical issues. The company needs to centralize and prioritize security findings across all AWS accounts and regions. (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Manually aggregate findings from each service and account into a spreadsheet for review.","Configure AWS Security Hub to aggregate findings from all linked accounts and enabled regions into a single console.","Develop custom AWS Lambda functions to pull findings from each service and store them in an Amazon S3 bucket.","Utilize AWS Security Hub to normalize and prioritize security findings based on industry standards like CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark.","Enable Amazon GuardDuty in all accounts and regions and review findings directly within the GuardDuty console for each account."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) and Option D (correct) describe the core capabilities of AWS Security Hub. It is designed to aggregate, normalize, and prioritize security findings from various AWS services and partner products across multiple accounts and regions, providing a centralized view and context for prioritization based on standards. Option A (incorrect) is a manual, error-prone, and unscalable approach. Option C (incorrect) involves custom development, which increases operational overhead and would need significant effort to replicate Security Hub''s normalization and prioritization features. Option E (incorrect) focuses only on GuardDuty and still requires reviewing findings in separate consoles, failing the centralization and prioritization requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain44_9',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_44_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_44_m_44_2_67',
  'A company hosts sensitive intellectual property on Amazon S3 and runs critical applications on Amazon EC2 instances. They are concerned about unauthorized access, suspicious network activity, and potential malware infections. They need a managed service that continuously monitors for threats without requiring agents or extensive configuration to proactively detect and alert on potential threats, suspicious activity, and malware in the AWS environment. (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Deploy a host-based intrusion detection system (HIDS) on all EC2 instances and configure custom alerts.","Enable Amazon GuardDuty to continuously monitor AWS account activity and network traffic for malicious behavior.","Implement AWS WAF on all application load balancers to filter out suspicious web requests.","Activate Amazon GuardDuty''s malware protection feature to scan Amazon S3 objects and EC2 instance volumes for malware.","Manually review AWS CloudTrail logs and VPC Flow Logs daily for suspicious patterns."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) and Option D (correct) correctly identify Amazon GuardDuty as the service that meets the requirements. Amazon GuardDuty provides continuous threat detection by monitoring AWS account activity and network traffic for malicious behavior, and its malware protection feature specifically scans Amazon S3 objects and EC2 instance volumes for malware, all without requiring agents. Option A (incorrect) requires deploying and managing agents, increasing operational overhead. Option C (incorrect) focuses on web application firewalling, which is important but doesn''t cover broader account activity, network threats, or malware detection on instances/S3. Option E (incorrect) is a manual and unscalable approach to threat detection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain44_9',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_44_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_44_m_44_2_68',
  'A company is migrating an on-premises application with a traditional perimeter firewall and host-based intrusion detection system to AWS. They want to replace these legacy security patterns with AWS-native controls that are more integrated, scalable, and automated, reducing the need for manual security management. (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Deploy third-party virtual firewalls on Amazon EC2 instances and manage them manually.","Utilize AWS Security Hub to aggregate security findings and automate responses across services.","Implement Amazon GuardDuty for continuous threat detection and monitoring of AWS accounts and network activity.","Maintain the existing on-premises firewall solution by extending it to the AWS VPC via VPN.","Configure AWS Config rules to enforce security best practices and automatically remediate non-compliant resources."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) and Option C (correct) represent AWS-native controls that replace legacy security patterns with integrated, scalable, and automated solutions. AWS Security Hub centralizes findings and enables automated responses, while Amazon GuardDuty provides continuous threat detection without manual agent management. Option A (incorrect) involves deploying and managing third-party virtual appliances, which increases operational overhead and may not be as integrated as native services. Option D (incorrect) maintains a legacy pattern and doesn''t fully leverage AWS-native security capabilities. Option E (incorrect) is for compliance enforcement and remediation, which is part of a broader security strategy, but GuardDuty and Security Hub are more direct replacements for IDS/firewall management in a cloud-native context.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain44_9',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_44_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_44_m_44_2_69',
  'A security engineer needs to create a new IAM policy that grants specific permissions to an application role but wants to ensure it adheres strictly to the principle of least privilege. Manually crafting these policies is complex and often leads to over-privileged roles. They need assistance in generating fine-grained policies based on access activity to enforce least privilege. (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Start with a full administrative policy and progressively remove permissions as errors occur.","Utilize AWS IAM Access Analyzer to generate policy recommendations based on actual access activity recorded in AWS CloudTrail.","Use AWS Config rules to audit existing policies for over-privilege, but not for generating new ones.","Leverage AWS IAM Access Analyzer to identify unused permissions in existing policies and refine them.","Manually write the policy by consulting AWS service documentation for each required action."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) and Option D (correct) both describe key functionalities of AWS IAM Access Analyzer for enforcing least privilege. It can analyze CloudTrail logs to generate policy recommendations based on actual usage, and it can identify unused permissions in existing policies, allowing for refinement. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern that starts with excessive permissions, violating the principle of least privilege. Option C (incorrect) is for auditing existing policies, not for generating new ones. Option E (incorrect) is a manual, time-consuming, and error-prone process that often results in over-privileged policies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain44_9',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_44_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_44_m_44_2_70',
  'A security operations center (SOC) receives thousands of security findings daily from various sources, making it challenging to identify the most critical issues requiring immediate attention. They need a solution that can aggregate these findings and help them prioritize based on severity and compliance standards to improve the efficiency of security operations. (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implement a custom SIEM solution to ingest all logs and findings, requiring significant development and maintenance.","Utilize AWS Security Hub to centralize security findings from multiple AWS services and partner solutions.","Configure Amazon GuardDuty to generate findings and then manually export them to a dashboard for prioritization.","Leverage AWS Security Hub''s integration with AWS standards (e.g., CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark) to normalize and prioritize findings.","Create Amazon CloudWatch alarms for individual security events and review each alarm manually."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) and Option D (correct) describe the core value proposition of AWS Security Hub. It centralizes findings from various sources and normalizes them into a standard format, allowing for prioritization based on severity and compliance standards like the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark, significantly improving SOC efficiency. Option A (incorrect) involves a custom SIEM, which has high development and maintenance overhead. Option C (incorrect) focuses only on GuardDuty and requires manual export, failing the aggregation and automated prioritization requirement. Option E (incorrect) creates too many individual alarms, leading to alert fatigue and making prioritization difficult.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain44_9',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_44_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_44_m_44_2_71',
  'A security team needs to aggregate security findings from various AWS services and prioritize them for remediation. Which AWS services are designed to centralize security posture management and threat detection? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Security Hub","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS IAM Access Analyzer","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Systems Manager Automation"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Security Hub) provides a comprehensive view of your security posture across your AWS accounts, aggregating security findings from multiple AWS services and third-party products. Amazon GuardDuty (Option B) is a threat detection service that continuously monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior, generating findings that are fed into Security Hub. AWS IAM Access Analyzer (Option C) helps identify unintended external access. Amazon CloudWatch (Option D) is for monitoring. AWS Systems Manager Automation (Option E) is for operational automation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain44_9',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_44_2',
  'medium'
);

