INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_44_m_44_3_64',
  'An Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database supporting a high-traffic analytics application is frequently experiencing slow query execution times, especially during report generation. The database administrator needs tools to analyze query plans, identify inefficient queries, and recommend indexing strategies to improve performance. The goal is to optimize database query performance and identify tuning opportunities for an Amazon RDS instance. (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Upgrade the Amazon RDS instance to a larger, more powerful instance type.","Use Amazon RDS Performance Insights to visualize database load and identify the most impactful SQL queries.","Migrate the database to a NoSQL solution like Amazon DynamoDB to avoid relational query issues.","Leverage Amazon CloudWatch metrics to monitor general database CPU and memory utilization.","Analyze query plans and execution statistics directly within Amazon RDS Performance Insights to pinpoint optimization areas."]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (correct) and Option E (correct) directly address database performance tuning. Amazon RDS Performance Insights provides a visual dashboard to understand database load, identify top SQL queries, and analyze execution statistics, which is crucial for pinpointing inefficient queries and informing indexing strategies. Option A (incorrect) is a common but often ineffective response (anti-pattern ap-3.3-001) if the bottleneck is not resource capacity but inefficient queries. Option C (incorrect) is a drastic architectural change that may not be necessary and doesn''t address the immediate need for tuning the existing relational database. Option D (incorrect) provides high-level metrics but lacks the granular database-specific insights needed for query optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain44_9',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_44_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_44_m_44_3_65',
  'A developer needs to understand the end-to-end latency of requests flowing through an AWS Lambda function, an Amazon API Gateway, and an Amazon DynamoDB table. They want a service that can provide a visual service map and detailed trace data for each request to visualize the request flow and identify latency issues across serverless components. (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use Amazon CloudWatch Logs to collect all logs and manually correlate timestamps across services.","Implement AWS X-Ray to instrument the Lambda function and enable tracing for API Gateway and DynamoDB.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch metrics for each service and create dashboards to monitor high-level performance.","Leverage AWS X-Ray to generate a service map and trace individual requests through the serverless architecture.","Deploy a custom APM agent within the Lambda function to send traces to a third-party tool."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) and Option D (correct) both describe the capabilities of AWS X-Ray, which is specifically designed for end-to-end tracing of requests across distributed applications, including serverless components like AWS Lambda, Amazon API Gateway, and Amazon DynamoDB. It provides a visual service map and detailed trace data, making it ideal for identifying latency issues. Option A (incorrect) involves manual correlation of logs, which is time-consuming and inefficient for complex request flows. Option C (incorrect) provides high-level metrics but lacks the granular, request-level tracing and service map visualization needed to pinpoint exact latency sources. Option E (incorrect) involves a custom agent and third-party tool, increasing operational overhead and potentially lacking deep integration with AWS services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain44_9',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_44_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_44_m_44_3_66',
  'A database administrator is troubleshooting an Amazon RDS MySQL instance that exhibits periodic CPU spikes and high active sessions. They need a granular view of database load, including SQL queries, hosts, and users, to diagnose the root cause without manually connecting to the database. The goal is to gain deep visibility into Amazon RDS database load and identify top SQL queries contributing to performance issues. (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Connect to the database instance via SSH and use native MySQL tools to monitor performance.","Utilize Amazon RDS Performance Insights to monitor database load and identify problematic SQL queries.","Increase the allocated storage IOPS for the Amazon RDS instance.","Leverage Amazon CloudWatch to monitor general CPU utilization and network activity of the RDS instance.","Examine the Performance Schema data directly through Amazon RDS Performance Insights to understand query execution."]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (correct) and Option E (correct) correctly identify Amazon RDS Performance Insights as the tool for deep database performance analysis. Performance Insights provides a visual dashboard that shows database load, top SQL queries, hosts, and users, allowing administrators to quickly identify the root cause of performance issues like CPU spikes and high active sessions without direct database access. Option A (incorrect) involves manual SSH access, which is not always available for RDS and is less efficient than a managed monitoring tool. Option C (incorrect) is a potential solution for I/O bottlenecks but doesn''t help diagnose query-related issues. Option D (incorrect) provides high-level metrics but lacks the granular database-specific insights needed for query optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain44_9',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_44_3',
  'hard'
);

