INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_72',
  'Arrange the following steps in the correct order to set up a basic AWS Lambda function.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure a trigger for the function (e.g., API Gateway, S3 event).","Define an IAM execution role with necessary permissions for Lambda.","Upload the deployment package (code and dependencies).","Create the AWS Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'The first step is to define the necessary IAM execution role (Option B) that the AWS Lambda function will assume to interact with other AWS services. Next, you create the AWS Lambda function itself (Option D), specifying the runtime and handler. After the function is created, you upload the actual code as a deployment package (Option C). Finally, you configure a trigger (Option A) that will invoke the Lambda function, such as an Amazon API Gateway endpoint or an Amazon S3 event.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_73',
  'Order the steps to deploy a containerized application using AWS Fargate on Amazon ECS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon ECS service to run and maintain tasks.","Define an Amazon ECS task definition.","Provision a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) and subnets.","Create an Amazon ECS cluster."]'::json,
  '{2,3,1,0}',
  'Before deploying any resources, a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) and its subnets (Option C) must be provisioned to provide network isolation. Then, an Amazon ECS cluster (Option D) is created to host the containerized applications. Next, an Amazon ECS task definition (Option B) is defined, which describes the containers, resources, and operating system. Finally, an Amazon ECS service (Option A) is created to maintain the desired number of tasks and handle deployments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_74',
  'Order the steps required to configure AWS Systems Manager State Manager to ensure consistent configuration across a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define a State Manager association, specifying the document, targets, and schedule.","Create or select an AWS Systems Manager document (SSM document) that defines the desired state.","Verify the compliance status of the managed instances in Systems Manager.","Ensure the EC2 instances have the SSM Agent installed and appropriate IAM permissions."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'The foundational step for using AWS Systems Manager is ensuring the target instances are ready. Option D (Ensure the EC2 instances have the SSM Agent installed and appropriate IAM permissions) is critical for the Systems Manager Agent to communicate with the service. Next, Option B (Create or select an AWS Systems Manager document (SSM document) that defines the desired state) specifies the configuration tasks to be performed. After defining the desired state, Option A (Define a State Manager association, specifying the document, targets, and schedule) links the SSM document to the target instances and sets the execution schedule. Finally, Option C (Verify the compliance status of the managed instances in Systems Manager) allows monitoring and auditing to confirm that the instances maintain the desired configuration. An incorrect order, such as defining an association before the SSM document exists, would lead to errors.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_75',
  'A solutions architect needs to set up a basic CI/CD pipeline for a serverless application using AWS CodePipeline. Order the fundamental steps to configure this pipeline.',
  'ordering',
  '["Add a build stage using AWS CodeBuild to compile code and create deployment artifacts.","Configure a source stage, typically linking to a code repository like AWS CodeCommit or GitHub.","Add a deploy stage using AWS CloudFormation or AWS Serverless Application Model (SAM) to deploy resources.","Define the pipeline structure and stages in AWS CodePipeline."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'The initial step in setting up any pipeline is to define its overall structure. Option D (Define the pipeline structure and stages in AWS CodePipeline) establishes the framework. Following this, Option B (Configure a source stage, typically linking to a code repository like AWS CodeCommit or GitHub) is essential to retrieve the application''s source code. Once the code is sourced, Option A (Add a build stage using AWS CodeBuild to compile code and create deployment artifacts) processes the code into deployable artifacts. Finally, Option C (Add a deploy stage using AWS CloudFormation or AWS Serverless Application Model (SAM) to deploy resources) takes these artifacts and deploys them to the AWS environment. Attempting to add stages before defining the pipeline or without a source would result in an incomplete or non-functional pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_76',
  'A company wants to implement a canary deployment for a new version of an API served by Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda. Order the steps to configure this deployment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the canary settings on the API Gateway stage to route a small percentage of traffic to the new Lambda version.","Deploy the new Lambda function version and associate it with a new API Gateway stage deployment.","Create a new version of the AWS Lambda function with the updated code.","Monitor the canary deployment for errors and performance, then gradually shift traffic or roll back."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'The process begins with preparing the new code. Option C (Create a new version of the AWS Lambda function with the updated code) ensures the new functionality is encapsulated. Next, Option B (Deploy the new Lambda function version and associate it with a new API Gateway stage deployment) makes the new version available through API Gateway. After the new version is deployed, Option A (Configure the canary settings on the API Gateway stage to route a small percentage of traffic to the new Lambda version) initiates the canary release by directing a subset of traffic. Finally, Option D (Monitor the canary deployment for errors and performance, then gradually shift traffic or roll back) is crucial for observing the impact and making informed decisions about increasing traffic or reverting the deployment. Deploying without creating a new Lambda version or monitoring would bypass the safety benefits of a canary release.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_77',
  'Order the steps to enable automated rollback for a CloudFormation stack update, ensuring stability in case of deployment failures.',
  'ordering',
  '["Update the CloudFormation stack with the new template, specifying the rollback configuration.","Create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm that monitors a critical metric for the application.","Define a rollback configuration for the CloudFormation stack, linking it to the CloudWatch alarm.","Ensure the CloudFormation service role has permissions to manage CloudWatch alarms and stack resources."]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}',
  'Before configuring any CloudFormation stack operations, the necessary permissions must be in place. Option D (Ensure the CloudFormation service role has permissions to manage CloudWatch alarms and stack resources) is the prerequisite. Next, Option B (Create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm that monitors a critical metric for the application) establishes the condition that will trigger a rollback. Following this, Option C (Define a rollback configuration for the CloudFormation stack, linking it to the CloudWatch alarm) associates the alarm with the stack''s rollback behavior. Finally, Option A (Update the CloudFormation stack with the new template, specifying the rollback configuration) executes the stack update with the rollback protection enabled. Attempting to update the stack or define rollback without the alarm or permissions would lead to errors or a lack of automated rollback capability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_78',
  'A solutions architect is deploying a new serverless application that consists of an API and backend logic. Order the steps to deploy this application using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create and deploy the Amazon API Gateway REST API, configuring routes and integration with the Lambda function.","Develop the application code and package it for deployment to AWS Lambda.","Create the AWS Lambda function, uploading the packaged code and configuring its execution role.","Test the deployed API Gateway endpoint to ensure the Lambda function is invoked correctly."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'The initial step is to prepare the application''s core logic. Option B (Develop the application code and package it for deployment to AWS Lambda) ensures the code is ready. Next, Option C (Create the AWS Lambda function, uploading the packaged code and configuring its execution role) provisions the serverless compute resource. Once the Lambda function is ready, Option A (Create and deploy the Amazon API Gateway REST API, configuring routes and integration with the Lambda function) exposes the Lambda function via an HTTP endpoint. Finally, Option D (Test the deployed API Gateway endpoint to ensure the Lambda function is invoked correctly) validates the end-to-end functionality. Attempting to create API Gateway routes before the Lambda function exists, or deploying without code, would result in an incomplete or non-functional application.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_79',
  'Order the steps to update an existing Amazon ECS service with a new task definition, ensuring minimal downtime.',
  'ordering',
  '["Monitor the deployment status and health of the new tasks.","Create a new task definition revision with the updated container image or configuration.","Update the ECS service, referencing the new task definition revision.","Ensure the ECS service''s deployment configuration is set for a rolling update strategy."]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'The first step is to define the changes. Option B (Create a new task definition revision with the updated container image or configuration) encapsulates the new version of the application. Next, Option D (Ensure the ECS service''s deployment configuration is set for a rolling update strategy) is a prerequisite to guarantee a smooth, minimal-downtime update. After the new task definition is ready and the deployment strategy is confirmed, Option C (Update the ECS service, referencing the new task definition revision) initiates the deployment. Finally, Option A (Monitor the deployment status and health of the new tasks) is crucial to verify the success and stability of the update. Updating the service without a new task definition or an appropriate deployment strategy would lead to errors or downtime.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_80',
  'A solutions architect wants to implement feature flagging for a web application using AWS AppConfig. Order the steps to set up a basic feature flag.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS AppConfig application and environment.","Deploy the configuration to the specified AppConfig environment.","Define a new configuration profile within the application, specifying a feature flag schema.","Integrate the AppConfig agent or SDK into the application to retrieve configuration."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'The foundational step for using AWS AppConfig is to set up the core resources. Option A (Create an AWS AppConfig application and environment) establishes the logical grouping for configurations. Next, Option C (Define a new configuration profile within the application, specifying a feature flag schema) creates the container for the feature flag data and its structure. After the profile is defined, Option B (Deploy the configuration to the specified AppConfig environment) publishes the feature flag values, making them available. Finally, Option D (Integrate the AppConfig agent or SDK into the application to retrieve configuration) enables the application to consume these feature flags at runtime. Attempting to define a profile without an application or deploy without a profile would lead to errors.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_81',
  'A financial services company is developing a new microservices-based application that processes sensitive customer transactions. The application uses AWS Lambda functions and Amazon SQS queues for asynchronous processing. The solutions architect needs to design a deployment strategy that allows for gradual rollout of new features to a small subset of users, enabling quick detection and rollback of issues, while minimizing risk to the entire user base. Arrange the following steps to implement a canary deployment for a new Lambda function version.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure an AWS Lambda alias to route a small percentage of traffic (e.g., 5%) to the new function version.","Create a new version of the AWS Lambda function with the updated code and configurations.","Monitor the new function version''s performance and error rates using Amazon CloudWatch.","Gradually increase the traffic percentage routed to the new function version until it reaches 100%, then decommission the old version."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Implementing a canary deployment for AWS Lambda involves a phased rollout. Option B (Create a new version of the AWS Lambda function with the updated code and configurations) is the initial step, as you need the new code to deploy. Option A (Configure an AWS Lambda alias to route a small percentage of traffic (e.g., 5%) to the new function version) follows, as this is how you introduce the canary. Option C (Monitor the new function version''s performance and error rates using Amazon CloudWatch) is crucial during the canary phase to detect any issues. Finally, Option D (Gradually increase the traffic percentage routed to the new function version until it reaches 100%, then decommission the old version) completes the rollout once confidence in the new version is established. This strategy allows for controlled exposure and rapid rollback.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_82',
  'A software development company is adopting a microservices architecture and needs to automate the deployment of containerized applications to Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate. The solutions architect must design a CI/CD pipeline that ensures repeatable, reliable, and automated deployments from source code commit to production. Arrange the following steps to establish a CI/CD pipeline for ECS Fargate using AWS developer tools.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure AWS CodeBuild to build the Docker image, run unit tests, and push the image to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR).","Set up AWS CodePipeline to orchestrate the entire release process, triggering on source code changes and integrating CodeBuild and CodeDeploy.","Create an Amazon ECS cluster and define the service and task definition for the application, specifying AWS Fargate as the launch type.","Configure AWS CodeDeploy to deploy the new container image to the Amazon ECS Fargate service, managing traffic shifting (e.g., blue/green)."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'Establishing a CI/CD pipeline for ECS Fargate starts with the foundational infrastructure. Option C (Create an Amazon ECS cluster and define the service and task definition for the application, specifying AWS Fargate as the launch type) is the prerequisite, as the deployment target must exist. Option A (Configure AWS CodeBuild to build the Docker image, run unit tests, and push the image to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR)) follows, handling the build and artifact storage. Option D (Configure AWS CodeDeploy to deploy the new container image to the Amazon ECS Fargate service, managing traffic shifting (e.g., blue/green)) then handles the actual deployment logic. Finally, Option B (Set up AWS CodePipeline to orchestrate the entire release process, triggering on source code changes and integrating CodeBuild and CodeDeploy) ties all these components together into an automated workflow. This sequence ensures infrastructure readiness, artifact creation, deployment, and overall orchestration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_83',
  'A startup is launching a new event-driven application that uses AWS Lambda functions and Amazon SQS queues. They anticipate frequent updates to the Lambda functions and need a deployment strategy that minimizes downtime and allows for quick rollbacks in case of issues. The solutions architect decides to implement a deployment strategy that gradually shifts traffic. Arrange the following steps to implement a safe, automated deployment for a new Lambda function version.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new version of the AWS Lambda function with the updated code.","Configure an AWS Lambda alias to point to the new function version, initially routing 0% traffic to it.","Use AWS CodeDeploy to gradually shift traffic from the old function version to the new one, monitoring metrics and alarms.","If issues are detected, automatically roll back traffic to the previous stable function version using CodeDeploy."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'A safe, automated deployment for AWS Lambda functions involves creating a new version, setting up an alias, and using CodeDeploy for controlled traffic shifting. Option A (Create a new version of the AWS Lambda function with the updated code) is the first step, as the new code needs to be available. Option B (Configure an AWS Lambda alias to point to the new function version, initially routing 0% traffic to it) prepares the alias for traffic shifting without immediately impacting users. Option C (Use AWS CodeDeploy to gradually shift traffic from the old function version to the new one, monitoring metrics and alarms) automates the controlled rollout. Finally, Option D (If issues are detected, automatically roll back traffic to the previous stable function version using CodeDeploy) ensures a safety net and quick recovery in case of problems. This sequence enables a robust and resilient deployment process.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_84',
  'A development team is building a new serverless application using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. They want to implement feature flagging to enable or disable specific features for different user segments without redeploying the entire application. The solutions architect needs to design a solution for feature flagging. Arrange the following steps to implement feature flagging using AWS AppConfig.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS AppConfig application, environment (e.g., ''Production''), and configuration profile for the feature flags.","Define the feature flags as JSON or YAML data within the AppConfig configuration profile, specifying their default states (e.g., enabled/disabled).","Integrate the AWS Lambda functions to retrieve the current feature flag configuration from AppConfig at runtime.","Deploy the application with the feature flag logic, then use AppConfig to update flag values in real-time, rolling out changes safely."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Implementing feature flagging with AWS AppConfig begins with setting up the AppConfig resources. Option A (Create an AWS AppConfig application, environment (e.g., ''Production''), and configuration profile for the feature flags) establishes the necessary AppConfig structure. Option B (Define the feature flags as JSON or YAML data within the AppConfig configuration profile, specifying their default states (e.g., enabled/disabled)) populates the configuration with the actual feature flags. Option C (Integrate the AWS Lambda functions to retrieve the current feature flag configuration from AppConfig at runtime) enables the application to read the flag states. Finally, Option D (Deploy the application with the feature flag logic, then use AppConfig to update flag values in real-time, rolling out changes safely) allows for dynamic control of features without code deployments. This sequence ensures AppConfig setup, flag definition, application integration, and dynamic management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_85',
  'A company is migrating its monolithic application to a microservices architecture on AWS. They want to leverage AWS Fargate for container orchestration to minimize operational overhead. The solutions architect needs to design a deployment strategy that allows for safe and automated rollouts of new microservice versions, with the ability to quickly revert if issues arise. Arrange the following steps to implement a blue/green deployment for an ECS Fargate service.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new Amazon ECS task definition and service for the updated application version (Green environment) in AWS Fargate.","Configure AWS CodeDeploy to manage the blue/green deployment, specifying the original (Blue) and new (Green) ECS task sets.","Monitor the Green environment for stability and performance using Amazon CloudWatch, and if stable, shift 100% of traffic.","If issues are detected during the deployment, use AWS CodeDeploy to automatically roll back to the original (Blue) environment."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Implementing a blue/green deployment for ECS Fargate starts with provisioning the new environment. Option A (Create a new Amazon ECS task definition and service for the updated application version (Green environment) in AWS Fargate) is the first step to prepare the new version. Option B (Configure AWS CodeDeploy to manage the blue/green deployment, specifying the original (Blue) and new (Green) ECS task sets) sets up the deployment orchestrator. Option C (Monitor the Green environment for stability and performance using Amazon CloudWatch, and if stable, shift 100% of traffic) is the critical monitoring and traffic shifting phase. Finally, Option D (If issues are detected during the deployment, use AWS CodeDeploy to automatically roll back to the original (Blue) environment) ensures a safety net and quick recovery. This sequence ensures new version creation, deployment orchestration, controlled rollout, and automated rollback.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_86',
  'A solutions architect is designing a blue/green deployment strategy for an application hosted on AWS Fargate behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB). Order the steps to perform a blue/green deployment using Amazon ECS and an ALB.',
  'ordering',
  '["Update the target group listener rule to shift traffic to the new green target group.","Deploy the new version of the application (green environment) to a new Amazon ECS service and target group.","Monitor the green environment for stability and performance.","Decommission the old blue environment and its associated resources."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'The correct sequence for a blue/green deployment involves first deploying the new version (green environment) alongside the existing (blue environment). Option B describes this initial deployment. Once the green environment is deployed, it must be thoroughly monitored and tested for stability and performance before traffic is shifted, as described in Option C. After successful validation, the ALB listener rule is updated to direct all traffic to the new green target group (Option A). Finally, once the green environment is fully operational and stable, the old blue environment can be safely decommissioned (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_87',
  'Order the typical steps involved in setting up a basic Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery (CI/CD) pipeline for an application deployed to AWS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy the application to the target AWS environment (e.g., AWS Fargate, AWS Lambda).","Build the application code and create artifacts (e.g., Docker image, ZIP file).","Store application source code in a version control system (e.g., AWS CodeCommit).","Run automated tests (unit, integration) against the built artifacts."]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}',
  'A CI/CD pipeline begins with source code management. Option C, storing source code in a version control system, is the foundational step. Next, the code is built into deployable artifacts, as described in Option B. After building, automated tests are crucial to ensure quality and functionality before deployment, which is Option D. Finally, if all tests pass, the application is deployed to the target environment, as stated in Option A.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_88',
  'A team wants to implement a canary deployment for a new feature in their serverless application using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. Order the steps to configure a canary release.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure API Gateway to route a small percentage of traffic to the new Lambda function version.","Create a new version of the AWS Lambda function with the new feature.","Monitor the new Lambda version for errors and performance metrics.","Gradually increase the traffic percentage to the new Lambda version until 100%."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The process starts by creating the new version of the AWS Lambda function that contains the new feature, as described in Option B. Once the new version is available, Amazon API Gateway is configured to route a small, controlled percentage of traffic to this new version, enabling the canary release (Option A). Continuous monitoring of the new version''s performance and error rates is critical during this phase to detect any issues early (Option C). If the new version performs as expected, the traffic can be gradually increased until all traffic is routed to the new version, completing the rollout (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_89',
  'Order the general steps to provision infrastructure using AWS CloudFormation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Review the created resources and outputs in the AWS Management Console or AWS CLI.","Define the desired AWS resources in a CloudFormation template (YAML or JSON).","Upload the template to an Amazon S3 bucket (optional, but good practice for larger templates).","Create a CloudFormation stack using the template."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'The first step in using AWS CloudFormation is to define the desired infrastructure in a template, as described in Option B. While optional for small templates, uploading the template to an Amazon S3 bucket (Option C) is a best practice for larger templates and provides versioning. Once the template is ready, a CloudFormation stack is created from it (Option D), which provisions the resources. Finally, after stack creation, the deployed resources and any defined outputs should be reviewed (Option A) to confirm successful deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_90',
  'A company is planning to migrate an on-premises application to AWS and wants to ensure a smooth transition with minimal downtime. Order the steps to implement a rolling deployment strategy for an application running on Amazon EC2 instances.',
  'ordering',
  '["Replace a small batch of old instances with new instances running the updated application version.","Monitor the newly updated instances for stability and performance.","Repeat the process of replacing batches and monitoring until all instances are updated.","Prepare the new application version and an updated Amazon Machine Image (AMI) or deployment package."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'Before any deployment, the new application version and its associated deployment artifacts (like an AMI) must be prepared, as stated in Option D. A rolling deployment then proceeds by updating a small batch of instances at a time (Option A), ensuring that the application remains available throughout the process. After each batch update, the newly updated instances must be carefully monitored for any issues (Option B). This cycle of updating a batch and monitoring is repeated until all instances are running the new version (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_91',
  'A development team wants to use feature flags to decouple feature releases from code deployments. Order the steps to implement feature flagging in an application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy the code with the new feature and the feature flag logic to production.","Implement conditional logic in the application code to enable or disable the feature based on the flag''s state.","Configure a feature flag service (e.g., AWS AppConfig, custom solution) to control the flag''s state.","Update the feature flag''s state in the service to enable the feature for specific users or all users."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'Implementing feature flagging starts with writing the conditional logic within the application code itself to respond to the flag''s state (Option B). Then, a mechanism to manage the flag''s state, such as a dedicated feature flag service, needs to be set up (Option C). Once the code is ready and the flag service is in place, the application code, including the feature flag logic, can be safely deployed to production with the feature initially disabled (Option A). Finally, when the team is ready to release the feature, they update the flag''s state in the feature flag service, activating the feature without requiring another code deployment (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_92',
  'Order the steps a solutions architect would take to set up a basic AWS CodePipeline for a web application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define a build stage to compile code and create artifacts using AWS CodeBuild.","Configure a source stage to pull code from a repository like AWS CodeCommit or GitHub.","Set up a deploy stage to push artifacts to a target environment like Amazon S3 or AWS Fargate.","Create the AWS CodePipeline and link the defined stages."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'A CodePipeline begins with the source stage, which retrieves the application code from a version control repository (Option B). Following the source stage, the code needs to be built and artifacts generated, which is handled in the build stage (Option A). After successful building, the artifacts are deployed to the target environment in the deploy stage (Option C). Finally, all these defined stages are integrated and linked together by creating the AWS CodePipeline itself (Option D).',
