  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_93',
  'A company wants to ensure that any changes to critical infrastructure are reviewed and approved. Order the steps to implement a change management process using AWS Systems Manager Change Manager.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define change templates and approval workflows within Change Manager.","Submit a change request for an infrastructure modification.","Approve the change request based on the defined workflow.","Execute the change and monitor its impact."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step in implementing a change management process is to define the rules and procedures for changes, which involves setting up change templates and approval workflows in Change Manager (Option A). Once the framework is in place, any proposed infrastructure modification must be submitted as a formal change request (Option B). This request then goes through the defined approval workflow, where stakeholders review and approve it (Option C). Only after approval can the change be executed, followed by monitoring to ensure it has the desired effect without adverse impact (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_94',
  'Order the steps to configure automated rollbacks for an AWS Lambda function deployment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define a CloudWatch alarm to monitor key metrics (e.g., error rate, invocations) for the new Lambda version.","Configure a deployment preference in AWS CodeDeploy for the Lambda function, specifying the alarm.","Deploy the new version of the AWS Lambda function.","If the alarm is triggered, AWS CodeDeploy automatically rolls back to the previous stable version."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To enable automated rollbacks, you first need a mechanism to detect issues. Option A describes defining a CloudWatch alarm to monitor the health of the new Lambda version. Next, you configure AWS CodeDeploy with deployment preferences for the Lambda function, linking it to the alarm, so CodeDeploy knows when to trigger a rollback (Option B). Then, the new Lambda function version is deployed (Option C). If the alarm thresholds are breached after deployment, CodeDeploy will automatically initiate a rollback to the last known good version (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_95',
  'Order the process for updating an Amazon ECS service with a new task definition version without downtime.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new task definition revision with the updated container image or configuration.","Update the Amazon ECS service to use the new task definition revision.","Monitor the deployment for health and stability of the new tasks.","The ECS service scheduler replaces old tasks with new tasks gradually."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'The first step is to create a new task definition revision that includes the desired updates, such as a new container image or environment variables (Option A). Once the new task definition is ready, the Amazon ECS service is updated to reference this new revision (Option B). Upon receiving the update, the ECS service scheduler begins to gradually replace the old tasks with new tasks running the updated definition, ensuring continuous availability (Option D). Throughout this process, it is critical to monitor the health and stability of the newly launched tasks to detect any issues (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_96',
  'A solutions architect is planning to update an application running on Amazon EC2 instances within an Auto Scaling group. Order the steps to perform an immutable deployment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new Auto Scaling group with the updated AMI and launch configuration/template.","Shift traffic from the old Auto Scaling group to the new one using a load balancer.","Monitor the new environment for stability and performance.","Terminate the old Auto Scaling group after successful cutover."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'An immutable deployment starts by creating an entirely new environment. Option A describes creating a new Auto Scaling group with the updated application version baked into a new AMI. After the new environment is provisioned, it must be thoroughly monitored and tested to ensure stability and performance (Option C). Once validated, traffic is shifted from the old environment to the new one, typically via a load balancer (Option B). Finally, if the cutover is successful, the old Auto Scaling group and its instances can be safely terminated (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_97',
  'Order the steps to configure AWS App Runner for deploying a containerized web application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the App Runner service with the source repository (e.g., ECR, GitHub) and deployment settings.","Deploy the containerized application to AWS App Runner.","Push the container image to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) or link to a source code repository.","Access the deployed application via the provided App Runner service URL."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'The first step for deploying a containerized application to AWS App Runner is to make the container image accessible, typically by pushing it to Amazon ECR or linking to a source code repository (Option C). Next, you configure the App Runner service, specifying the source, compute, and networking settings (Option A). After configuration, App Runner automatically builds and deploys the application (Option B). Finally, once deployed, the application can be accessed through the unique service URL provided by App Runner (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_98',
  'A development team needs to deploy a new serverless API using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. They want to automate the deployment process. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to set up this serverless API.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the Amazon API Gateway to create an API endpoint and integrate it with the AWS Lambda function.","Write the application code for the API logic and package it for deployment.","Deploy the AWS Lambda function code and configure its execution role and memory settings.","Test the API endpoint to ensure the Lambda function is invoked correctly and returns the expected response."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'The logical first step in deploying a serverless API is to develop the application logic. Option B (Write the application code for the API logic and package it for deployment) is where the core functionality is created. Once the code is ready, it needs to be deployed as a serverless function. Option C (Deploy the AWS Lambda function code and configure its execution role and memory settings) handles the creation and configuration of the compute resource. After the Lambda function is deployed, it needs to be exposed via an API endpoint. Option A (Configure the Amazon API Gateway to create an API endpoint and integrate it with the AWS Lambda function) sets up the public interface. Finally, after all components are in place, the API should be validated. Option D (Test the API endpoint to ensure the Lambda function is invoked correctly and returns the expected response) confirms the end-to-end functionality.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_99',
  'A company is migrating an existing monolithic application to a containerized microservice running on Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate. The team wants to ensure a controlled rollout. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to deploy a new version of an ECS service with a rolling update.',
  'ordering',
  '["Update the ECS service with the new task definition, specifying the desired count.","Create a new task definition revision that points to the updated container image.","Monitor the deployment progress and health of the new tasks.","Build and push the new container image to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR)."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'The process of deploying a new version of a containerized application starts with preparing the new image. Option D (Build and push the new container image to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR)) ensures the updated application code is available. Next, a new definition for how this image will run is needed. Option B (Create a new task definition revision that points to the updated container image) specifies the container configuration. Once the new task definition is ready, the ECS service needs to be updated to use it. Option A (Update the ECS service with the new task definition, specifying the desired count) initiates the rolling update, where new tasks are launched and old ones are terminated gradually. Finally, it''s crucial to verify the deployment''s success. Option C (Monitor the deployment progress and health of the new tasks) ensures the new version is stable and functioning correctly.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_1_100',
  'A solutions architect needs to establish a basic Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery (CI/CD) pipeline for an application hosted on AWS. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to set up a foundational CI/CD pipeline using AWS services.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure AWS CodeBuild to compile code, run tests, and create deployment artifacts.","Set up a source code repository in AWS CodeCommit and commit the application code.","Define an AWS CodePipeline to orchestrate the build and deployment stages.","Configure AWS CodeDeploy to deploy the application artifacts to the target environment."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'The initial step in establishing a CI/CD pipeline is to have the source code managed in a repository. Option B (Set up a source code repository in AWS CodeCommit and commit the application code) provides the foundation. Once the code is in the repository, it needs to be built and tested. Option A (Configure AWS CodeBuild to compile code, run tests, and create deployment artifacts) handles this part of the CI process. After the build artifacts are ready, they need to be deployed to the target environment. Option D (Configure AWS CodeDeploy to deploy the application artifacts to the target environment) manages the deployment. Finally, to automate and connect these stages, a pipeline orchestrator is needed. Option C (Define an AWS CodePipeline to orchestrate the build and deployment stages) ties all these components together into an automated workflow.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_1',
  'medium'
);

