INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_49',
  'A solutions architect needs to implement a pilot light disaster recovery strategy for an application running on Amazon EC2. Order the steps to set up this strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Periodically test the recovery process by launching full capacity in the DR Region.","Replicate critical data (e.g., database backups, S3 objects) to the disaster recovery (DR) Region.","Provision minimal core infrastructure (e.g., VPC, subnets, AMIs) in the DR Region.","Create automation (e.g., AWS CloudFormation, AWS Lambda) to quickly provision full application stack in DR Region."]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}',
  'The initial step for a pilot light strategy is to establish the minimal infrastructure in the DR Region. Option C (Provision minimal core infrastructure (e.g., VPC, subnets, AMIs) in the DR Region) sets up the ''pilot light''. Next, Option B (Replicate critical data (e.g., database backups, S3 objects) to the disaster recovery (DR) Region) ensures that essential data is available for recovery. Following this, Option D (Create automation (e.g., AWS CloudFormation, AWS Lambda) to quickly provision full application stack in DR Region) prepares for rapid scaling during a disaster. Finally, Option A (Periodically test the recovery process by launching full capacity in the DR Region) is crucial to validate the strategy''s effectiveness. Replicating data before the DR infrastructure is ready, or not having automation, would hinder rapid recovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_50',
  'Order the steps to configure Amazon RDS for Multi-AZ deployment to enhance database availability.',
  'ordering',
  '["Monitor the RDS instance for failover events and performance metrics.","Create a new Amazon RDS database instance, selecting the Multi-AZ deployment option.","Ensure the VPC has subnets configured across at least two Availability Zones.","Configure security groups and network ACLs to allow application access to the RDS instance."]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}',
  'The prerequisite for Multi-AZ deployment is a properly configured network. Option C (Ensure the VPC has subnets configured across at least two Availability Zones) provides the necessary infrastructure. Next, Option B (Create a new Amazon RDS database instance, selecting the Multi-AZ deployment option) provisions the database with high availability enabled. After the instance is created, Option D (Configure security groups and network ACLs to allow application access to the RDS instance) ensures connectivity for the application. Finally, Option A (Monitor the RDS instance for failover events and performance metrics) is essential for operational oversight. Attempting to create a Multi-AZ instance without sufficient subnets or without configuring network access would lead to errors or connectivity issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_51',
  'A solutions architect needs to set up a warm standby disaster recovery solution for a web application running on Amazon EC2 and Amazon RDS. Order the steps to implement this strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure Amazon Route 53 to fail over DNS records to the DR Region endpoint during a disaster.","Deploy a scaled-down version of the application infrastructure (EC2 instances, ALB) in the DR Region.","Replicate the Amazon RDS database from the primary Region to the DR Region using a read replica or Global Database.","Ensure the VPC and networking are correctly configured in both the primary and DR Regions."]'::json,
  '{3,2,1,0}',
  'The foundational step for any multi-Region strategy is proper networking. Option D (Ensure the VPC and networking are correctly configured in both the primary and DR Regions) provides the necessary connectivity. Next, Option C (Replicate the Amazon RDS database from the primary Region to the DR Region using a read replica or Global Database) ensures data consistency and availability in the DR Region. Following this, Option B (Deploy a scaled-down version of the application infrastructure (EC2 instances, ALB) in the DR Region) establishes the ''warm standby'' environment. Finally, Option A (Configure Amazon Route 53 to fail over DNS records to the DR Region endpoint during a disaster) enables traffic redirection during an actual event. Deploying infrastructure or replicating data without proper networking would lead to a non-functional DR solution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_52',
  'Order the steps to enable automated backups for an Amazon DynamoDB table to ensure data durability and point-in-time recovery.',
  'ordering',
  '["Verify that point-in-time recovery (PITR) is enabled for the DynamoDB table.","Create the Amazon DynamoDB table with appropriate capacity settings.","Configure on-demand backups or continuous backups (PITR) for the table.","Monitor backup status and retention policies in the DynamoDB console."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'The first step is to create the resource that needs to be backed up. Option B (Create the Amazon DynamoDB table with appropriate capacity settings) establishes the table. Next, Option C (Configure on-demand backups or continuous backups (PITR) for the table) enables the backup functionality. Following this, Option A (Verify that point-in-time recovery (PITR) is enabled for the DynamoDB table) confirms the continuous backup capability. Finally, Option D (Monitor backup status and retention policies in the DynamoDB console) ensures ongoing data protection and compliance. Attempting to configure backups on a non-existent table or not verifying PITR would lead to incomplete or failed backup strategies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_53',
  'A solutions architect needs to implement cross-Region failover for a web application using Amazon Route 53. Order the steps to configure this failover mechanism.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create Route 53 health checks for the primary and secondary endpoints.","Deploy the application infrastructure in both the primary and secondary AWS Regions.","Configure a Route 53 hosted zone for the application''s domain.","Create Route 53 DNS records (e.g., failover routing policy) pointing to the application endpoints."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'The initial step for cross-Region failover is to ensure the application is available in both regions. Option B (Deploy the application infrastructure in both the primary and secondary AWS Regions) establishes the endpoints. Next, Option C (Configure a Route 53 hosted zone for the application''s domain) provides the DNS management service. After the hosted zone is ready, Option A (Create Route 53 health checks for the primary and secondary endpoints) enables Route 53 to monitor the health of the application. Finally, Option D (Create Route 53 DNS records (e.g., failover routing policy) pointing to the application endpoints) configures the failover logic based on health checks. Configuring DNS records or health checks without deployed infrastructure or a hosted zone would be ineffective.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_54',
  'Order the steps to restore an Amazon EBS snapshot to a new Amazon EC2 instance.',
  'ordering',
  '["Launch a new EC2 instance and attach the newly created EBS volume.","Create an EBS volume from the desired EBS snapshot in the same Availability Zone.","Identify the specific EBS snapshot to be restored.","Connect to the EC2 instance and mount the attached EBS volume."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'The first step in any restoration process is to identify the source. Option C (Identify the specific EBS snapshot to be restored) ensures the correct data is used. Next, Option B (Create an EBS volume from the desired EBS snapshot in the same Availability Zone) provisions the storage with the restored data. After the volume is created, Option A (Launch a new EC2 instance and attach the newly created EBS volume) provides the compute resource and connects the storage. Finally, Option D (Connect to the EC2 instance and mount the attached EBS volume) makes the data accessible within the operating system. Attempting to create a volume or launch an instance without identifying the snapshot would be impossible or lead to incorrect data restoration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_55',
  'A solutions architect is designing a multi-site active-active architecture for a global application. Order the conceptual steps to implement this highly resilient design.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure global load balancing (e.g., Amazon Route 53 with latency routing or AWS Global Accelerator) to distribute traffic.","Deploy identical application stacks, including compute, database, and storage, in multiple AWS Regions.","Implement data synchronization or replication mechanisms between the databases and storage across Regions.","Establish robust cross-Region network connectivity and security controls."]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}',
  'The foundation for a multi-Region active-active architecture is secure and reliable inter-Region connectivity. Option D (Establish robust cross-Region network connectivity and security controls) is the initial step. Next, Option B (Deploy identical application stacks, including compute, database, and storage, in multiple AWS Regions) ensures the application is fully operational in each desired region. Following this, Option C (Implement data synchronization or replication mechanisms between the databases and storage across Regions) is crucial for maintaining data consistency across all active sites. Finally, Option A (Configure global load balancing (e.g., Amazon Route 53 with latency routing or AWS Global Accelerator) to distribute traffic) directs user requests to the nearest or healthiest active site. Deploying applications or configuring load balancing without proper network connectivity or data synchronization would result in an unstable or inconsistent global application.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_56',
  'A global e-commerce platform needs to ensure continuous business operations even in the event of a regional AWS outage. The platform uses Amazon RDS for its primary database and Amazon S3 for static content and user-uploaded files. The solutions architect must design a multi-Region disaster recovery strategy with an RPO of minutes and an RTO of less than an hour, using a warm standby approach. Arrange the following steps to establish this disaster recovery strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure Amazon Route 53 to use health checks and failover routing policies to direct traffic to the DR Region upon primary Region failure.","Set up cross-Region replication for Amazon S3 buckets to continuously synchronize static content and user-uploaded files to the DR Region.","Deploy a scaled-down version of the application servers (e.g., EC2 instances or ECS Fargate tasks) in the DR Region, configured to connect to the DR database.","Establish Amazon RDS cross-Region read replicas in the disaster recovery (DR) Region, configured for asynchronous replication from the primary database."]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}',
  'For a warm standby multi-Region DR strategy, the data replication must be established first. Option D (Establish Amazon RDS cross-Region read replicas in the disaster recovery (DR) Region, configured for asynchronous replication from the primary database) ensures the database is continuously updated. Option B (Set up cross-Region replication for Amazon S3 buckets to continuously synchronize static content and user-uploaded files to the DR Region) ensures all static data is also replicated. Option C (Deploy a scaled-down version of the application servers (e.g., EC2 instances or ECS Fargate tasks) in the DR Region, configured to connect to the DR database) provisions the standby compute resources. Finally, Option A (Configure Amazon Route 53 to use health checks and failover routing policies to direct traffic to the DR Region upon primary Region failure) enables automated traffic redirection in case of a disaster. This order ensures data consistency and readiness for failover.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_57',
  'A media company stores vast amounts of video content in Amazon S3. They need to implement a robust data backup and recovery strategy that ensures data durability and availability, while also being cost-effective for long-term archival. The solutions architect needs to configure lifecycle policies and replication to meet these requirements. Arrange the following steps to implement a comprehensive backup and recovery solution for Amazon S3 objects.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure S3 Versioning on the source bucket to protect against accidental deletions or overwrites.","Implement S3 Lifecycle policies to transition older, less frequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval or S3 Glacier Deep Archive for cost optimization.","Enable S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) to an independent S3 bucket in a different AWS Region for disaster recovery and compliance.","Regularly test the data recovery process by restoring objects from both versioned backups and Glacier archives to ensure data integrity and RTO objectives are met."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'A comprehensive S3 backup and recovery strategy starts with fundamental protection. Option A (Configure S3 Versioning on the source bucket to protect against accidental deletions or overwrites) is the foundational step for data protection within the same bucket. Option C (Enable S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) to an independent S3 bucket in a different AWS Region for disaster recovery and compliance) provides geographical redundancy and isolation. Option B (Implement S3 Lifecycle policies to transition older, less frequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval or S3 Glacier Deep Archive for cost optimization) is then applied to manage storage costs over time. Finally, Option D (Regularly test the data recovery process by restoring objects from both versioned backups and Glacier archives to ensure data integrity and RTO objectives are met) is essential to validate the entire strategy and ensure recoverability. This sequence ensures data protection, redundancy, cost optimization, and verified recovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_58',
  'A global SaaS provider needs to ensure their application remains available and performant even during major regional outages. The application is built on Amazon EC2, Amazon RDS, and Amazon S3. The solutions architect must design a multi-Region active-passive disaster recovery strategy with an RTO of under 15 minutes and an RPO of under 5 minutes. Arrange the following steps to implement a robust multi-Region failover solution using Route 53.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure Amazon Route 53 with health checks for the primary Region''s endpoints and a failover routing policy to direct traffic to the DR Region upon health check failure.","Establish cross-Region replication for Amazon S3 buckets to continuously synchronize static content and user-uploaded data to the DR Region.","Deploy a scaled-down but operational set of EC2 instances and an Amazon RDS read replica in the DR Region, configured to connect to the replicated data.","Implement a mechanism to promote the Amazon RDS read replica in the DR Region to a standalone primary instance during a failover event."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'Implementing a multi-Region active-passive DR strategy requires careful sequencing of data replication, infrastructure provisioning, and failover automation. Option B (Establish cross-Region replication for Amazon S3 buckets to continuously synchronize static content and user-uploaded data to the DR Region) ensures data consistency for static assets. Option C (Deploy a scaled-down but operational set of EC2 instances and an Amazon RDS read replica in the DR Region, configured to connect to the replicated data) provisions the warm standby infrastructure. Option D (Implement a mechanism to promote the Amazon RDS read replica in the DR Region to a standalone primary instance during a failover event) is critical for database recovery in the DR Region. Finally, Option A (Configure Amazon Route 53 with health checks for the primary Region''s endpoints and a failover routing policy to direct traffic to the DR Region upon health check failure) automates the traffic redirection to the DR site. This order ensures data readiness, infrastructure availability, database recovery, and automated traffic switchover.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_59',
  'A large enterprise needs to ensure that its critical applications can quickly recover from data corruption or accidental deletion, with minimal data loss. The applications store data in Amazon S3. The solutions architect needs to design a robust data protection strategy that includes versioning and replication. Arrange the following steps to implement a comprehensive data protection strategy for Amazon S3 objects.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable S3 Versioning on the source bucket to keep multiple variants of an object, protecting against accidental overwrites and deletions.","Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) or Same-Region Replication (SRR) to an independent target bucket, ideally in a separate account, for disaster recovery.","Implement S3 Object Lock in compliance mode for critical data to prevent objects from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed amount of time or indefinitely.","Regularly audit S3 bucket policies and IAM permissions to ensure least privilege access and prevent unauthorized modifications to data or replication configurations."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'A comprehensive data protection strategy for Amazon S3 involves multiple layers. Option A (Enable S3 Versioning on the source bucket to keep multiple variants of an object, protecting against accidental overwrites and deletions) is the foundational step for object recovery within the same bucket. Option B (Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) or Same-Region Replication (SRR) to an independent target bucket, ideally in a separate account, for disaster recovery) provides geographical or logical isolation for disaster recovery. Option C (Implement S3 Object Lock in compliance mode for critical data to prevent objects from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed amount of time or indefinitely) adds an immutable layer for regulatory compliance. Finally, Option D (Regularly audit S3 bucket policies and IAM permissions to ensure least privilege access and prevent unauthorized modifications to data or replication configurations) is crucial for maintaining the security posture of the entire solution. This sequence builds a robust, multi-layered data protection strategy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_60',
  'A global e-commerce platform needs to ensure its critical customer-facing services remain available even during a major regional outage. The application uses Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon RDS, and Amazon S3. The solutions architect must design a multi-Region disaster recovery strategy with an RPO of minutes and an RTO of less than an hour, using a pilot light approach. Arrange the following steps to establish this disaster recovery strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure Amazon Route 53 with health checks and failover routing policies to direct traffic to the DR Region upon primary Region failure.","Establish Amazon RDS cross-Region read replicas in the disaster recovery (DR) Region, configured for asynchronous replication from the primary database.","Set up cross-Region replication for Amazon S3 buckets to continuously synchronize static content and user-uploaded files to the DR Region.","Deploy minimal, core infrastructure (e.g., a few EC2 instances, load balancers) in the DR Region, but keep them stopped or scaled down to a minimal state."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'For a pilot light multi-Region DR strategy, data replication is paramount. Option B (Establish Amazon RDS cross-Region read replicas in the disaster recovery (DR) Region, configured for asynchronous replication from the primary database) ensures the database is continuously updated. Option C (Set up cross-Region replication for Amazon S3 buckets to continuously synchronize static content and user-uploaded files to the DR Region) ensures all static data is also replicated. Option D (Deploy minimal, core infrastructure (e.g., a few EC2 instances, load balancers) in the DR Region, but keep them stopped or scaled down to a minimal state) provisions the ''pilot light'' compute resources. Finally, Option A (Configure Amazon Route 53 with health checks and failover routing policies to direct traffic to the DR Region upon primary Region failure) enables automated traffic redirection in case of a disaster. This order ensures data consistency, minimal standby infrastructure, and readiness for failover.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_61',
  'A company needs to implement a pilot light disaster recovery strategy for its application hosted on Amazon EC2 and Amazon RDS in a primary AWS Region. Order the steps to set up this strategy in a secondary Region.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure cross-Region replication for the Amazon RDS database.","Deploy a minimal set of core infrastructure (e.g., Amazon EC2 AMIs, network configurations) in the secondary Region.","Regularly test the recovery process by launching full capacity in the secondary Region.","Keep the replicated RDS instance in the secondary Region in a stopped or minimal state."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'A pilot light strategy begins by deploying a minimal, scaled-down version of the infrastructure in the secondary Region, including pre-configured AMIs and network settings (Option B). Data replication is crucial, so setting up cross-Region replication for the Amazon RDS database is the next step (Option A). To keep costs low in the pilot light state, the replicated RDS instance should be kept in a stopped or minimal state (Option D). Regular testing of the recovery process by fully launching the application in the secondary Region is essential to ensure readiness (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_62',
  'A solutions architect needs to design a warm standby disaster recovery solution for a web application running on Amazon EC2 and Amazon RDS. Order the steps to implement this strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure cross-Region replication for the Amazon RDS database.","Deploy a scaled-down but functional version of the application in the secondary Region.","Set up Amazon Route 53 DNS failover to redirect traffic to the secondary Region during a disaster.","Regularly synchronize non-replicated data and test failover procedures."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'A warm standby strategy starts by deploying a scaled-down but functional version of the application in the secondary Region, ready to be scaled up (Option B). Data consistency is critical, so configuring cross-Region replication for the Amazon RDS database is the next logical step (Option A). While the RDS database handles continuous replication, any other non-replicated data or configuration changes should be regularly synchronized, and failover procedures must be tested to ensure readiness (Option D). Finally, Amazon Route 53 DNS failover is configured to automatically or manually redirect traffic to the secondary Region when a disaster occurs (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_63',
  'Order the steps to configure Amazon Route 53 for DNS failover between two environments in different AWS Regions.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create health checks for the primary and secondary endpoints.","Create a failover routing policy in Route 53, associating the health checks with the primary record.","Define primary and secondary DNS records (e.g., A records) pointing to the respective application endpoints.","Test the failover by simulating a failure of the primary endpoint."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'Before configuring failover, you need to define the endpoints for both primary and secondary environments. Option C describes creating the primary and secondary DNS records pointing to these endpoints. To enable automatic failover, health checks must be created for both endpoints to monitor their availability (Option A). Next, a failover routing policy is configured in Route 53, linking the health checks to the primary record (Option B). Finally, it''s crucial to test the failover mechanism by simulating a failure of the primary endpoint to ensure it behaves as expected (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_64',
  'A company needs to back up its Amazon EBS volumes for disaster recovery. Order the steps to automate EBS snapshot creation and retention.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager (DLM) policy.","Define the target Amazon EBS volumes using tags.","Specify the snapshot creation schedule and retention rules in the DLM policy.","Verify that snapshots are being created and retained according to the policy."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To automate EBS snapshot management, you first need to identify the target EBS volumes. Option B describes defining these volumes, typically using tags. Next, an Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager (DLM) policy is created (Option A), which will govern the snapshot process. Within the DLM policy, you specify the schedule for snapshot creation and the rules for their retention (Option C). Finally, after the policy is active, it''s important to verify that snapshots are being created and retained as configured (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_65',
  'Order the steps to configure Amazon RDS Multi-AZ for high availability.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the ''Multi-AZ deployment'' option when creating or modifying an Amazon RDS instance.","Choose the desired database engine, instance class, and storage.","Monitor the RDS instance for automatic failover events.","Verify that the standby replica is provisioned in a different Availability Zone."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'Configuring Amazon RDS Multi-AZ begins with selecting the core database parameters like engine, instance class, and storage (Option B). The critical step for Multi-AZ is to explicitly select the ''Multi-AZ deployment'' option during instance creation or modification (Option A). After this, you should verify that RDS has provisioned a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone (Option D). Finally, it''s important to monitor the RDS instance, particularly during maintenance or simulated failures, to observe automatic failover events (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_66',
  'A company needs to ensure business continuity for its serverless application using AWS Lambda and Amazon DynamoDB. Order the steps to implement a cross-Region active-passive disaster recovery strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy a duplicate set of AWS Lambda functions and API Gateway endpoints in the secondary Region.","Configure Amazon DynamoDB global tables for cross-Region data replication.","Set up Amazon Route 53 DNS failover to switch traffic to the secondary Region upon primary failure.","Regularly test the failover process and application functionality in the secondary Region."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'For an active-passive serverless DR strategy, data replication is paramount. Option B, configuring Amazon DynamoDB global tables, ensures data is replicated across Regions. Next, the serverless application components, such as AWS Lambda functions and API Gateway endpoints, need to be deployed in the secondary Region (Option A). To manage traffic redirection during a disaster, Amazon Route 53 DNS failover must be configured (Option C). Finally, regular testing of the failover process and application functionality in the secondary Region is crucial to ensure readiness (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_67',
  'Order the steps to configure a basic backup and restore strategy for an Amazon EC2 instance using Amazon EBS snapshots.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon EBS snapshot of the EC2 instance''s root volume.","Launch a new EC2 instance from the created snapshot.","Stop the EC2 instance (optional, but recommended for application-consistent snapshots).","Verify that the new EC2 instance is running with the restored data."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'For an application-consistent backup, it''s recommended to stop the EC2 instance before taking a snapshot (Option C). After the instance is stopped (or if taking a crash-consistent snapshot of a running instance), an Amazon EBS snapshot of its root volume is created (Option A). To restore, a new EC2 instance is launched using this snapshot as the root volume (Option B). Finally, you must verify that the newly launched EC2 instance is functioning correctly with the restored data (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_68',
  'A company wants to use Amazon SQS and AWS Lambda to decouple microservices and handle message processing during bursts. Order the steps to set up this architecture.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the AWS Lambda function to process messages from the SQS queue.","Create an Amazon SQS standard queue.","Set up the Lambda function''s trigger to be the SQS queue.","Send messages to the SQS queue from the producing microservice."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The first step is to create the Amazon SQS standard queue, which will act as the buffer for messages (Option B). Next, you need to develop and configure the AWS Lambda function that will consume and process these messages (Option A). The crucial link is setting up the SQS queue as the trigger for the Lambda function, so Lambda automatically invokes when messages arrive (Option C). Finally, the producing microservice can start sending messages to the SQS queue, which will then be processed by Lambda (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_69',
  'Order the steps to configure Amazon EventBridge to route events from an AWS service to an AWS Lambda function.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Lambda function to process the events.","Create an EventBridge rule that matches specific event patterns from the source service.","Add the Lambda function as a target for the EventBridge rule.","Verify that events are being captured and processed by the Lambda function."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The process begins with creating the AWS Lambda function that will be responsible for processing the incoming events (Option A). Next, an Amazon EventBridge rule is created to define which events from a source service should be captured (Option B). The Lambda function is then added as a target to this EventBridge rule, ensuring that matched events are sent to it (Option C). Finally, it''s important to verify that events are correctly flowing from the source, through EventBridge, and being processed by the Lambda function (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_70',
  'A solutions architect wants to implement a multi-site active-active disaster recovery strategy for a global application. Order the general steps to achieve this.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy the full application stack in multiple AWS Regions.","Configure global data replication (e.g., Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables, cross-Region RDS Read Replicas).","Use Amazon Route 53 latency-based or geolocation routing to direct users to the closest healthy Region.","Implement continuous synchronization and automated failover mechanisms."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'An active-active strategy starts by deploying the complete application stack in multiple AWS Regions (Option A) to serve traffic concurrently. Next, global data replication must be configured to ensure data consistency across all active Regions (Option B). Continuous synchronization of application state and robust automated failover mechanisms are essential for seamless operation and resilience (Option D). Finally, Amazon Route 53 is used to intelligently route user traffic to the closest or healthiest active Region (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_71',
  'A company needs to implement a multi-Region active-passive disaster recovery strategy for its web application, with Amazon Route 53 managing failover. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to configure this DR solution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure health checks for the primary and secondary endpoints in Amazon Route 53.","Deploy the web application infrastructure in a secondary AWS Region.","Create Route 53 failover routing policies pointing to the primary and secondary endpoints.","Ensure data replication is configured between the primary and secondary Regions (e.g., for databases or S3 buckets)."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'The first step in setting up a multi-Region active-passive disaster recovery strategy is to establish the recovery site. Option B (Deploy the web application infrastructure in a secondary AWS Region) ensures that a standby environment is available. Once the infrastructure is deployed, data consistency between regions is critical. Option D (Ensure data replication is configured between the primary and secondary Regions (e.g., for databases or S3 buckets)) establishes the RPO. Next, Route 53 needs to know the health of each endpoint to perform failover. Option A (Configure health checks for the primary and secondary endpoints in Amazon Route 53) enables this monitoring. Finally, the routing logic for failover is defined. Option C (Create Route 53 failover routing policies pointing to the primary and secondary endpoints) directs traffic based on health check status.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_72',
  'A solutions architect needs to implement a robust backup and restore strategy for critical data stored in Amazon S3. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to configure S3 object versioning and cross-Region replication for disaster recovery.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable S3 Versioning on both the source and destination S3 buckets.","Create a destination S3 bucket in a different AWS Region.","Configure a cross-Region replication (CRR) rule on the source bucket, specifying the destination bucket.","Create an IAM role that grants S3 replication permissions to the source bucket."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'To set up S3 object versioning and cross-Region replication, the destination for the replicated data must exist first. Option B (Create a destination S3 bucket in a different AWS Region) is the foundational step. Next, versioning is a prerequisite for replication and a best practice for data protection. Option A (Enable S3 Versioning on both the source and destination S3 buckets) ensures objects are versioned. For S3 to perform replication, it needs appropriate permissions. Option D (Create an IAM role that grants S3 replication permissions to the source bucket) provides the necessary authorization. Finally, the replication rule can be configured. Option C (Configure a cross-Region replication (CRR) rule on the source bucket, specifying the destination bucket) initiates the automatic replication process.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_73',
  'A company requires a warm standby disaster recovery solution for its critical application running on Amazon EC2 instances. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to implement a warm standby solution in a different AWS Region.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure data replication (e.g., database backups, S3 replication) from the primary Region to the standby Region.","Deploy a scaled-down version of the application infrastructure (e.g., smaller EC2 instances, fewer instances) in the secondary Region.","Create Amazon Machine Images (AMIs) of the primary EC2 instances for quick deployment in the standby Region.","Set up Amazon Route 53 failover routing policies to direct traffic to the standby Region upon disaster."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'The initial step for a warm standby solution is to prepare the application''s base images for quick deployment. Option C (Create Amazon Machine Images (AMIs) of the primary EC2 instances for quick deployment in the standby Region) ensures that the application environment can be rapidly provisioned. Next, the standby infrastructure needs to be deployed, albeit in a scaled-down capacity. Option B (Deploy a scaled-down version of the application infrastructure (e.g., smaller EC2 instances, fewer instances) in the secondary Region) establishes the warm standby environment. Data consistency is crucial for recovery. Option A (Configure data replication (e.g., database backups, S3 replication) from the primary Region to the standby Region) ensures the standby environment has up-to-date data. Finally, to automate the failover, a routing mechanism is needed. Option D (Set up Amazon Route 53 failover routing policies to direct traffic to the standby Region upon disaster) ensures traffic redirection during an outage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_43_m_43_2_74',
  'A solutions architect needs to implement cross-Region replication for an Amazon DynamoDB table to enhance business continuity. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to configure a DynamoDB global table.',
  'ordering',
  '["Ensure the DynamoDB table has DynamoDB Streams enabled with the ''New and old images'' view type.","Create the initial DynamoDB table in the primary AWS Region.","Add additional AWS Regions to the global table configuration in the DynamoDB console or via API.","Verify that the table''s write capacity units (WCUs) and read capacity units (RCUs) are adequately provisioned for replication traffic."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The first step in creating a DynamoDB global table is to have the base table. Option B (Create the initial DynamoDB table in the primary AWS Region) establishes the table that will be replicated. Global tables rely on DynamoDB Streams for replication. Option A (Ensure the DynamoDB table has DynamoDB Streams enabled with the ''New and old images'' view type) is a prerequisite for enabling global tables. Once the primary table and its stream are ready, additional regions can be added. Option C (Add additional AWS Regions to the global table configuration in the DynamoDB console or via API) expands the table to other regions. Finally, to ensure smooth operation, especially under heavy load, resource provisioning should be verified. Option D (Verify that the table''s write capacity units (WCUs) and read capacity units (RCUs) are adequately provisioned for replication traffic) helps prevent throttling and performance issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain43_9',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_43_2',
  'medium'
);

