INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_1_70',
  'A Chief Financial Officer (CFO) at a large enterprise is skeptical about the financial benefits of migrating their on-premises data centers to AWS. The solutions architect needs to prepare a compelling argument that goes beyond just infrastructure costs, demonstrating the full economic impact and operational advantages of the cloud. Which two key components should be emphasized when developing a business case and Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) analysis for the migration? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Option A: Focus solely on the direct cost comparison of server hardware and software licenses between on-premises and AWS.","Option B: Include the operational cost savings from reduced data center management, power, cooling, and network infrastructure.","Option C: Quantify the business value derived from increased agility, faster time-to-market, and improved scalability for new initiatives.","Option D: Exclude the cost of new cloud-native skill acquisition and training, assuming existing staff can adapt immediately.","Option E: Present a TCO model that only considers a rehost strategy, as it''s the simplest to estimate."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) is crucial for a comprehensive TCO analysis. Cloud migration significantly reduces operational costs associated with physical data centers, including power, cooling, physical security, and network infrastructure, as well as the labor involved in managing these. Option C (correct) addresses the business value, which is often a major driver for cloud adoption. Increased agility, faster time-to-market for new products, and the ability to scale rapidly translate into significant competitive advantages and revenue opportunities that must be quantified in a robust business case. Option A (incorrect) is too narrow; focusing solely on direct hardware and software costs overlooks significant operational and business benefits, leading to an incomplete and potentially misleading TCO. Option D (incorrect) is a common oversight. Training and upskilling staff for cloud environments are essential investments and must be included in the TCO analysis to provide a realistic financial picture. Option E (incorrect) aligns with anti-pattern ap-4.1-001. A TCO model should evaluate various migration strategies (rehost, replatform, refactor) to find the optimal path, not default to rehost, especially when seeking broader benefits.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_1_71',
  'A company is conducting a portfolio assessment of its 150 on-premises applications to determine their suitability for cloud migration. Some applications are critical but have strict regulatory compliance requirements tied to specific on-premises hardware, while others are rarely used and have outdated technology. The solutions architect needs to recommend strategies that optimize the migration effort and cost by addressing these diverse application profiles. Which two 7 Rs migration strategies should be applied in this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Option A: Rehost all applications to Amazon EC2 to achieve the fastest migration timeline.","Option B: Retire applications that are no longer needed or provide minimal business value.","Option C: Retain applications on-premises if they have unique regulatory or technical constraints that make cloud migration impractical or excessively costly.","Option D: Refactor all applications into serverless microservices to maximize cloud-native benefits.","Option E: Repurchase commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) software for all legacy applications."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) is the ''Retire'' strategy. For applications that are rarely used or provide minimal business value, retiring them eliminates unnecessary migration effort and ongoing costs, optimizing the overall portfolio. Option C (correct) is the ''Retain'' strategy. Applications with strict regulatory compliance tied to specific on-premises hardware, or those with prohibitive migration costs, might be best kept on-premises. This acknowledges that not every workload is suitable for immediate cloud migration. Option A (incorrect) is the ''Rehost'' strategy for all applications. While quick, it doesn''t address the goal of optimizing effort for diverse profiles and can lead to migrating technical debt, especially for outdated or unnecessary applications (anti-pattern ap-4.1-001). Option D (incorrect) is the ''Refactor'' strategy for all applications. While beneficial for modernization, refactoring every application is a massive undertaking, potentially impractical for all 150 applications, especially those that are rarely used. Option E (incorrect) is the ''Repurchase'' strategy. While valid for some COTS applications, it''s not a universal solution for all legacy applications and might not be suitable for custom-built systems.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_1_72',
  'A company''s migration team has proposed a ''lift and shift'' strategy for all 300 on-premises applications to meet an aggressive 12-month timeline. While this approach offers speed, many of these applications are monolithic, tightly coupled, and have accumulated significant technical debt over years. The business leadership is concerned that this strategy will not fully leverage AWS''s capabilities and may hinder future innovation. Which two actions should the solutions architect recommend to address these concerns and align with AWS best practices? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Option A: Proceed with the rehost strategy for all applications to ensure the aggressive timeline is met, deferring modernization efforts.","Option B: Conduct a detailed workload assessment to identify applications that are good candidates for replatforming or refactoring.","Option C: Prioritize refactoring for well-architected applications that would significantly benefit from cloud-native services like AWS Lambda or Amazon ECS.","Option D: Utilize AWS Application Migration Service (MGN) for all migrations, as it is designed for rapid rehosting.","Option E: Focus on migrating only the databases first, leaving the application layer on-premises for a phased approach."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) is essential. A detailed workload assessment helps determine the most appropriate ''R'' (rehost, replatform, refactor, etc.) for each application, ensuring that the migration strategy aligns with business goals and technical characteristics. This avoids the anti-pattern of defaulting to rehost for all workloads. Option C (correct) emphasizes prioritizing modernization for suitable applications. Well-architected applications, or those with clear benefits from cloud-native patterns, should be refactored or replatformed to realize the full potential of AWS, such as improved scalability, agility, and reduced operational overhead. Option A (incorrect) directly embodies anti-pattern ap-4.1-001, which states that defaulting to rehost for all workloads minimizes migration effort but moves technical debt to the cloud without realizing cloud-native benefits. Option D (incorrect) suggests using AWS Application Migration Service (MGN) for all migrations. While MGN is excellent for rehosting, using it for *all* migrations without considering other strategies like replatforming or refactoring would perpetuate the anti-pattern. Option E (incorrect) proposes migrating only databases first. While a phased approach can be good, this specific phase might not address the core issue of application modernization and could introduce complex hybrid dependencies without a clear overall strategy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_1_73',
  'A company is planning to migrate its on-premises applications to AWS and needs to gain visibility into its current server inventory, application dependencies, and resource utilization. Which AWS services can assist in collecting this information for migration planning? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Application Discovery Service","AWS DataSync","AWS Migration Hub","AWS Database Migration Service (DMS)","AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT)"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (AWS Application Discovery Service) is correct because it helps identify applications running in on-premises data centers, their dependencies, and performance characteristics. Option C (AWS Migration Hub) is correct because it provides a single location to track the progress of application migrations across multiple AWS and partner solutions, and it integrates with AWS Application Discovery Service for discovery. Option B (AWS DataSync) is incorrect as it is used for online data transfer between on-premises storage and AWS storage services, not for application discovery. Option D (AWS Database Migration Service (DMS)) is incorrect as it is specifically for migrating databases. Option E (AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT)) is incorrect as it is used to convert database schemas from one engine to another.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_1_74',
  'When evaluating existing workloads for migration to AWS, a Solutions Architect must consider various strategies, often referred to as the ''7 Rs of migration''. Which of the following are valid migration strategies within this framework? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Rehost","Re-evaluate","Replatform","Refactor","Re-architect"]'::json,
  '{0,2,3}',
  'Option A (Rehost) is correct. Also known as ''lift and shift'', this strategy involves moving applications without making any changes. Option C (Replatform) is correct. This involves making some cloud-native optimizations to achieve a tangible benefit, but without changing the core architecture of the application. Option D (Refactor) is correct. This involves re-imagining how an application is architected and developed, typically using cloud-native features to improve agility, scalability, and performance. Option B (Re-evaluate) is not one of the standard 7 Rs of migration. Option E (Re-architect) is often used interchangeably with Refactor, but ''Refactor'' is the more precise term within the 7 Rs framework.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_1_75',
  'Before initiating a large-scale migration, a company needs to centralize its migration planning and track the progress of various migration activities across different tools. They also require a service to discover detailed information about their on-premises server inventory. Which AWS services would fulfill these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Migration Hub","AWS DMS","AWS Application Discovery Service","AWS Snow Family","AWS DataSync"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (AWS Migration Hub) is correct because it provides a single location to track the progress of application migrations across multiple AWS and partner solutions, centralizing planning and tracking. Option C (AWS Application Discovery Service) is correct because it helps collect detailed information about on-premises servers, including inventory, configuration, and performance data, which is crucial for migration planning and populating Migration Hub. Option B (AWS DMS) is incorrect as it is for database migration, not general discovery or centralized tracking of all migration activities. Option D (AWS Snow Family) is incorrect as it is for offline data transfer. Option E (AWS DataSync) is incorrect as it is for online data transfer between on-premises and AWS storage, not for discovery or centralized migration tracking.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_1',
  'medium'
);

