INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_78',
  'Order the steps to perform an offline data transfer of large datasets using AWS Snowball Edge.',
  'ordering',
  '["Ship the Snowball Edge device back to AWS","Create a job in the AWS Snow Family console","Transfer data from your on-premises storage to the Snowball Edge device","Receive the Snowball Edge device"]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'The process begins by creating a job in the AWS Snow Family console (Option B), specifying the data to be transferred. After the device is prepared, you receive the Snowball Edge device (Option D) at your location. Then, transfer data from your on-premises storage to the Snowball Edge device (Option C). Finally, ship the Snowball Edge device back to AWS (Option A) for data import into Amazon S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_79',
  'A company plans to migrate a legacy SQL Server database to Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. Order the high-level process using AWS migration tools.',
  'ordering',
  '["Perform schema conversion and code transformation using AWS SCT","Set up AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) for data replication","Assess the source database for migration complexity","Cut over to the Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance"]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'For a heterogeneous database migration, first assess the source database for migration complexity (Option C), often using AWS SCT''s assessment report. Next, perform schema conversion and code transformation using AWS SCT (Option A) to convert SQL Server objects to PostgreSQL. Then, set up AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) for data replication (Option B) to move the actual data. Finally, cut over to the Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance (Option D) once replication is complete and validated.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_80',
  'Order the steps to assess a database for heterogeneous migration compatibility using AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT).',
  'ordering',
  '["Connect SCT to the source and target databases","Generate a database migration assessment report","Create a new AWS SCT project","Review the assessment report for conversion complexity"]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'To assess a database with SCT, first create a new AWS SCT project (Option C). Next, connect SCT to both the source and target databases (Option A) to establish communication. Then, generate a database migration assessment report (Option B), which analyzes the compatibility. Finally, review the assessment report for conversion complexity (Option D) and identify any manual conversion efforts required.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_81',
  'A remote branch office with limited network connectivity needs to transfer a large amount of backup data to AWS. Order the steps for using AWS DataSync.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure a DataSync task with the source and destination locations","Deploy and activate an AWS DataSync agent in the remote office","Create an Amazon S3 bucket as the destination","Start the DataSync task and monitor progress"]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'First, deploy and activate an AWS DataSync agent in the remote office (Option B) to facilitate secure data transfer from the local storage. Next, create an Amazon S3 bucket (Option C) in AWS to serve as the destination for the backup data. Then, configure a DataSync task (Option A), specifying the on-premises location (via the agent) and the S3 bucket. Finally, start the DataSync task and monitor its progress (Option D) to ensure successful data transfer.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_82',
  'A company needs to migrate 50 Windows servers from its on-premises data center to AWS with minimal downtime, as these servers host critical business applications. The solutions architect has chosen AWS Application Migration Service (MGN) for its automated, block-level replication capabilities. The architect must outline the precise sequence of steps to execute this rehost migration efficiently. Order the following steps for a rehost migration using AWS Application Migration Service (MGN).',
  'ordering',
  '["Launch test instances on AWS from the replicated data to validate application functionality and performance.","Install the AWS Replication Agent on each source Windows server to initiate block-level data replication to a staging area in AWS.","Configure the replication settings for each source server within the AWS MGN console, specifying target instance types and subnet.","Perform a cutover to the newly launched AWS instances during a planned maintenance window, redirecting traffic to the cloud environment."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'The migration process with AWS MGN begins with setting up replication. Option B (correct) involves installing the AWS Replication Agent on source servers, which starts the data replication. Following this, Option C (correct) configures the replication settings in the MGN console, defining how the instances will be provisioned in AWS. Once replication is stable, Option A (correct) launches test instances to validate the migrated applications without impacting production. Finally, Option D (correct) performs the cutover during a maintenance window, switching production traffic to the AWS instances. Incorrect ordering would lead to replication issues, unvalidated migrations, or unplanned downtime.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_83',
  'A development team needs to migrate a production MySQL database to Amazon RDS for MySQL with minimal downtime, as the application relying on it is critical. The solutions architect has decided to use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) for an online, continuous replication migration. A precise sequence of configuration and execution steps is required to ensure data integrity and a smooth transition. Order the following steps for an online database migration using AWS DMS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a DMS replication instance in AWS to perform the data migration.","Configure source and target endpoints in DMS, specifying connection details for the on-premises MySQL database and the Amazon RDS instance.","Create and start a DMS migration task, selecting full load plus change data capture (CDC) for continuous replication.","Monitor the replication task, validate data consistency, and plan a cutover window to switch application traffic to the Amazon RDS instance."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step in an AWS DMS migration is to provision the necessary infrastructure. Option A (correct) involves creating a DMS replication instance, which is the compute resource for the migration. Next, Option B (correct) configures the source and target endpoints, defining where DMS will read from and write to. After endpoints are set up, Option C (correct) creates and starts the DMS migration task, initiating the data transfer and continuous replication. Finally, Option D (correct) monitors the task and plans the cutover, ensuring data consistency before switching applications. Incorrect ordering would prevent DMS from connecting to databases or initiating replication properly.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_84',
  'A company is migrating a legacy Oracle database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL to leverage open-source benefits and reduce licensing costs. The existing schema and stored procedures are highly customized for Oracle, requiring significant conversion effort. The solutions architect has chosen AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT) to automate much of this process. A clear sequence of operations is needed to ensure a successful schema and code conversion. Order the following steps for schema conversion using AWS SCT.',
  'ordering',
  '["Connect AWS SCT to both the source Oracle database and the target Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL instance.","Install and configure AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT) on a client machine or EC2 instance.","Analyze the source schema to identify conversion complexities and generate a database migration assessment report.","Convert the schema and database code (e.g., stored procedures, functions) within SCT and apply it to the target Aurora PostgreSQL database."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The process begins with setting up the tool itself. Option B (correct) involves installing and configuring AWS SCT. Once SCT is ready, Option A (correct) connects it to both the source and target databases, establishing communication. After connections are made, Option C (correct) analyzes the source schema, providing insights into conversion complexity. Finally, Option D (correct) converts the schema and applies it to the target database, completing the core task. Incorrect ordering would mean SCT cannot connect to databases or perform analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_85',
  'A research institution needs to migrate 500 TB of archival scientific data from a remote on-premises facility with extremely limited internet connectivity to Amazon S3. The data transfer must be secure and efficient, and traditional online methods are not feasible due to bandwidth constraints. The solutions architect has identified AWS Snowball Edge as the optimal solution. Order the following steps for a large-scale offline data migration using AWS Snowball Edge.',
  'ordering',
  '["Connect the AWS Snowball Edge device to the on-premises network and transfer the 500 TB of data from local storage to the device.","Order an AWS Snowball Edge device through the AWS Management Console, specifying the desired capacity and S3 bucket for data import.","Ship the filled Snowball Edge device back to AWS.","Monitor the data import process as AWS uploads the data from the device to the designated Amazon S3 bucket."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The process starts with requesting the device. Option B (correct) involves ordering an AWS Snowball Edge device through the console. Once the device arrives, Option A (correct) connects it to the local network and transfers the data onto it. After data transfer is complete, Option C (correct) ships the device back to AWS. Finally, Option D (correct) monitors the data import as AWS transfers the data from the device to Amazon S3. Incorrect ordering would mean the device isn''t ordered, data isn''t transferred, or the device isn''t returned for import.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_86',
  'A media company needs to migrate 100 TB of NFS share data, including video assets and associated metadata, from its on-premises storage to Amazon S3. The migration must preserve file metadata and be performed securely and efficiently over the network. The solutions architect has selected AWS DataSync for this task due to its ability to handle large transfers and maintain data integrity. Order the following steps for migrating file data from on-premises to Amazon S3 using AWS DataSync.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy an AWS DataSync agent on-premises in a virtual machine or EC2 instance.","Create a DataSync task, specifying the source NFS location and the target Amazon S3 bucket.","Configure the source location (NFS share) and the destination location (Amazon S3 bucket) within DataSync.","Execute the DataSync task and monitor its progress through the AWS Management Console."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'The first step for AWS DataSync is to establish connectivity to the on-premises environment. Option A (correct) involves deploying an AWS DataSync agent on-premises. Once the agent is deployed, Option C (correct) configures both the source NFS location and the target Amazon S3 location within DataSync. With locations defined, Option B (correct) creates the DataSync task, linking the source and destination. Finally, Option D (correct) executes the task and monitors its progress. Incorrect ordering would prevent the agent from being available or the locations from being defined before creating the task.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_87',
  'A critical production database migration is planned from on-premises SQL Server to Amazon RDS for SQL Server. The solutions architect is acutely aware of the risks associated with database migrations, especially data integrity, and needs to ensure a robust fallback plan is in place to mitigate potential issues during cutover, as per anti-pattern ap-4.2-001. Order the following steps for a database migration with a comprehensive fallback plan.',
  'ordering',
  '["Perform a full data validation between the source and target databases in parallel with ongoing replication.","Set up continuous data replication from the on-premises source database to the Amazon RDS target using AWS DMS.","Plan a detailed cutover window, including a tested rollback procedure to revert to the source database if issues arise.","Execute the cutover during the planned window, switching application traffic to the Amazon RDS instance, and perform final validation."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To ensure a safe database migration with a fallback, the process begins with establishing replication. Option B (correct) sets up continuous data replication using AWS DMS, ensuring the target database is kept up-to-date. Concurrently with replication, Option A (correct) performs full data validation to ensure consistency. Before cutover, Option C (correct) plans a detailed cutover window that explicitly includes a tested rollback procedure, which is crucial for mitigating risks. Finally, Option D (correct) executes the cutover and performs final validation. An incorrect order, particularly skipping validation or rollback planning, would embody the anti-pattern of attempting online migration without a fallback plan.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_88',
  'A company wants to modernize a legacy monolithic Java application by containerizing it and deploying it to Amazon ECS. The application has several dependencies and a complex build process. The solutions architect needs to define a clear sequence of steps to successfully containerize the application and prepare it for deployment to AWS. Order the following steps for containerizing a legacy application for migration to Amazon ECS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Analyze the application''s runtime environment, dependencies, and external configurations to identify containerization requirements.","Build a Docker image for the application, ensuring all dependencies are included and the application starts correctly.","Create a Dockerfile that defines the application''s environment, dependencies, and startup commands.","Test the Docker image locally to ensure the application functions as expected within the container before deploying to Amazon ECS."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'The first step in containerization is understanding the application. Option A (correct) involves analyzing the application''s environment and dependencies. Based on this analysis, Option C (correct) creates a Dockerfile, which is the blueprint for the container image. With the Dockerfile, Option B (correct) builds the Docker image. Finally, Option D (correct) tests the Docker image locally to validate its functionality before pushing it to a registry or deploying it to ECS. Incorrect ordering would lead to building images without proper definitions or deploying untested containers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_89',
  'A small business needs to migrate an on-premises SQL Server database to Amazon RDS for SQL Server. The database is not excessively large, and a simple, one-time migration method is preferred to minimize complexity. The solutions architect has decided to use the native SQL Server backup and restore functionality, leveraging Amazon S3 as an intermediate storage. Order the following steps for migrating a SQL Server database to Amazon RDS for SQL Server using native backup/restore.',
  'ordering',
  '["Generate a full backup of the on-premises SQL Server database.","Create an Amazon S3 bucket in the same AWS Region as the target Amazon RDS instance.","Upload the SQL Server backup file from on-premises to the created Amazon S3 bucket.","Restore the database backup from Amazon S3 to the Amazon RDS for SQL Server instance and configure application connectivity."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The migration process begins by preparing the target environment for the backup file. Option B (correct) creates an Amazon S3 bucket, which will serve as the temporary storage for the backup. Next, Option A (correct) generates a full backup of the on-premises database. Once the backup is created, Option C (correct) uploads the backup file to the S3 bucket. Finally, Option D (correct) restores the database from S3 to the Amazon RDS instance and configures application connectivity. Incorrect ordering would mean the S3 bucket isn''t ready or the backup isn''t available for restoration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_90',
  'A company plans to rehost several on-premises Windows servers to AWS using AWS Application Migration Service (MGN). Order the typical steps for performing a rehost migration with MGN.',
  'ordering',
  '["Install the AWS Replication Agent on the source servers.","Configure replication settings and launch test instances.","Perform a cutover to the newly launched AWS instances.","Monitor data replication and ensure synchronization."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}',
  'The process begins by installing the AWS Replication Agent (Option A) on the source servers to enable data replication. After installation, monitoring data replication (Option D) ensures data is continuously synchronized. Next, replication settings are configured, and test instances are launched (Option B) to validate the migrated servers. Finally, a cutover (Option C) is performed to switch production traffic to the AWS instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_2_91',
  'A development team needs to convert an on-premises Oracle database schema to PostgreSQL for migration to Amazon RDS. Order the typical steps for using AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT).',
  'ordering',
  '["Connect AWS SCT to the source Oracle database and the target PostgreSQL database.","Analyze the source schema for compatibility issues and conversion complexity.","Apply the converted schema to the target PostgreSQL database.","Convert the schema objects from Oracle to PostgreSQL using SCT."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'The process starts by connecting AWS SCT to both the source and target databases (Option A). After connection, SCT analyzes the source schema (Option B) to identify any conversion challenges. Next, SCT is used to convert the schema objects (Option D) from the source to the target format. Finally, the converted schema is applied (Option C) to the target PostgreSQL database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
