  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_3_79',
  'A Solutions Architect needs to ensure an Amazon ECS task has the necessary permissions to interact with other AWS services. Order the steps to configure IAM roles.',
  'ordering',
  '["Attach the IAM policy to the created IAM role","Create an IAM policy with the required permissions","Specify the IAM role in the Amazon ECS task definition","Create an IAM role for the ECS task"]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'First, create an IAM policy (Option B) that defines the specific permissions required by the ECS task. Next, create an IAM role for the ECS task (Option D), which will assume this policy. Then, attach the IAM policy to the created IAM role (Option A). Finally, specify this IAM role in the Amazon ECS task definition (Option C) so that any tasks launched with this definition inherit these permissions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_3_80',
  'Order the steps to scale out the number of tasks for an Amazon ECS service.',
  'ordering',
  '["Update the desired count of tasks for the ECS service","Monitor the new tasks for successful launch and health","Identify the Amazon ECS service to scale","Ensure sufficient cluster capacity (for EC2 launch type)"]'::json,
  '{2,3,0,1}',
  'To scale an ECS service, first identify the Amazon ECS service to scale (Option C). If using the EC2 launch type, ensure sufficient cluster capacity (Option D) is available for the new tasks. Then, update the desired count of tasks for the ECS service (Option A) to the new, higher number. Finally, monitor the new tasks for successful launch and health (Option B) to confirm the scaling operation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_3_81',
  'A development team is deploying a new microservice application that needs to run in a serverless container environment to minimize operational overhead. The solutions architect has chosen Amazon ECS with the Fargate launch type. The team needs a clear sequence of steps to get their containerized application running on this platform. Order the following steps to deploy a containerized application to Amazon ECS Fargate.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define an Amazon ECS Task Definition that specifies the Docker image, CPU, memory, and networking requirements for the application.","Create an Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) repository for the application''s Docker images.","Build the Docker image for the application and push it to the Amazon ECR repository.","Create an Amazon ECS Service to run and maintain the desired number of tasks (containers) based on the Task Definition using the Fargate launch type."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'The deployment process for containerized applications typically starts with image management. Option B (correct) creates an Amazon ECR repository to store the Docker images. Next, Option C (correct) builds the Docker image and pushes it to the ECR repository. Once the image is available, Option A (correct) defines an Amazon ECS Task Definition, which describes how the container should run. Finally, Option D (correct) creates an Amazon ECS Service to deploy and manage the tasks based on the Task Definition. Incorrect ordering would mean the image isn''t available or the task definition isn''t created before attempting to deploy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_3_82',
  'A team of data scientists needs shared file storage that can be accessed concurrently by a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances for processing large datasets. The solution must be scalable, highly available, and provide a familiar file system interface. The solutions architect has decided to use Amazon EFS. Order the following steps to implement shared file storage for a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon EFS file system, specifying performance mode and throughput mode.","Configure EFS mount targets in the desired subnets (e.g., across multiple Availability Zones for high availability).","Attach appropriate security groups to the EFS mount targets to control network access.","Mount the Amazon EFS file system onto each Amazon EC2 instance using the NFS client."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step is to provision the file system itself. Option A (correct) involves creating the Amazon EFS file system. Once the file system exists, Option B (correct) configures EFS mount targets in the desired subnets, making it accessible within the VPC. After mount targets are created, Option C (correct) attaches security groups to control network access to these targets. Finally, Option D (correct) mounts the EFS file system onto the EC2 instances, making the storage available to applications. Incorrect ordering would mean the file system isn''t created or isn''t accessible before attempting to mount.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_3_83',
  'A company is replacing its aging on-premises Elasticsearch cluster with a managed AWS service to reduce operational burden and improve scalability. The solutions architect has chosen Amazon OpenSearch Service. The team needs a clear sequence of steps to provision the new OpenSearch domain and migrate existing data. Order the following steps to migrate an on-premises search cluster to Amazon OpenSearch Service.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain, configuring instance types, storage, and network access (VPC or public).","Migrate existing data from the on-premises Elasticsearch cluster to the new OpenSearch Service domain using tools like Logstash or custom scripts.","Configure fine-grained access control and security policies for the OpenSearch Service domain.","Update application endpoints to point to the new Amazon OpenSearch Service domain."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'The migration process begins with provisioning the target service. Option A (correct) involves creating the Amazon OpenSearch Service domain. Once the domain is created, Option C (correct) configures fine-grained access control and security policies, which is critical for securing the service. After security is established, Option B (correct) migrates the data from the on-premises cluster to the new domain. Finally, Option D (correct) updates application endpoints to direct traffic to the new OpenSearch Service domain. Incorrect ordering, such as migrating data before security is configured, would be a security risk.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_3_84',
  'A media archive company stores petabytes of data in Amazon S3. The data is frequently accessed for a short period after upload, then infrequently accessed for several months, and finally becomes rarely accessed archival data. The solutions architect needs to optimize storage costs by automatically transitioning data between S3 storage classes based on access patterns. Order the following steps to optimize Amazon S3 storage for cost-effectiveness for varying access patterns.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define an S3 Lifecycle Policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days.","Define an S3 Lifecycle Policy to transition objects from S3 Standard-IA to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days.","Upload new objects to Amazon S3 Standard storage class.","Define an S3 Lifecycle Policy to transition objects from S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 365 days."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'The process begins with the initial ingestion of data. Option C (correct) involves uploading new objects to Amazon S3 Standard, which is suitable for frequently accessed data. Following the initial period, Option A (correct) defines a Lifecycle Policy to move objects to S3 Standard-IA for infrequently accessed data. As access patterns further decrease, Option B (correct) defines a Lifecycle Policy to transition objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval for archival purposes. Finally, for long-term, rarely accessed archives, Option D (correct) defines a Lifecycle Policy to move objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive. Incorrect ordering of lifecycle policies would lead to suboptimal cost savings or data being moved to the wrong tier prematurely.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_3_85',
  'A new gaming application requires a low-latency, high-throughput database that can handle millions of requests per second with a flexible schema. The solutions architect has determined that Amazon DynamoDB is the most suitable choice. The team needs a clear sequence of steps to set up and configure this scalable NoSQL database. Order the following steps to set up a scalable, high-performance NoSQL database for a new application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Design the DynamoDB table schema, including primary key (partition key and sort key) and any necessary secondary indexes.","Create the Amazon DynamoDB table, specifying the designed schema and initial read/write capacity modes (on-demand or provisioned).","Implement the application logic to interact with the DynamoDB table, including data insertion, retrieval, and updates.","Conduct performance testing and monitor DynamoDB metrics to ensure the table meets the application''s throughput and latency requirements."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step in setting up a database is always schema design. Option A (correct) involves designing the DynamoDB table schema, including primary keys and indexes. Once the schema is designed, Option B (correct) creates the DynamoDB table based on that schema. After the table is provisioned, Option C (correct) implements the application logic to interact with the table. Finally, Option D (correct) conducts performance testing to validate that the database meets the application''s requirements. Incorrect ordering, such as creating the table before designing the schema, would lead to inefficient or incorrect database structures.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_3_86',
  'A company needs to migrate an on-premises Windows file server to AWS, requiring native Server Message Block (SMB) protocol support and seamless integration with their existing Active Directory for user authentication. The solutions architect has chosen Amazon FSx for Windows File Server. A precise sequence of steps is needed to set up this managed file server on AWS. Order the following steps to implement a Windows file server on AWS with native SMB support.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Directory Service for Microsoft Active Directory (or connect to an existing self-managed AD) to provide authentication.","Create an Amazon FSx for Windows File Server file system, linking it to the AWS Directory Service domain.","Migrate data from the on-premises Windows file server to the new FSx for Windows File Server instance.","Update client machines or applications to map network drives to the new Amazon FSx for Windows File Server endpoint."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'For a Windows file server with Active Directory integration, the directory service is foundational. Option A (correct) involves creating or connecting to AWS Directory Service for Microsoft Active Directory. Once the directory is in place, Option B (correct) creates the Amazon FSx for Windows File Server, linking it to the Active Directory. After the file system is provisioned, Option C (correct) migrates data from the on-premises server. Finally, Option D (correct) updates client mappings to point to the new FSx endpoint. Incorrect ordering, such as creating FSx before AD, would prevent proper integration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_3_87',
  'A small startup is deploying its first web application on AWS, which will initially run on a single Amazon EC2 instance. The solutions architect needs to guide the team through the fundamental steps of provisioning and configuring this instance to host the application, ensuring it has the correct operating system and network access. Order the following steps to provision and configure an Amazon EC2 instance for a web application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Choose an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) that includes the desired operating system and any pre-installed software.","Select an appropriate Amazon EC2 instance type based on compute, memory, and networking requirements.","Configure network settings, including the VPC, subnet, and security groups to allow necessary inbound/outbound traffic (e.g., HTTP/HTTPS).","Launch the Amazon EC2 instance and then install the web application software and any required dependencies."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The process of launching an EC2 instance starts with selecting the base image. Option A (correct) involves choosing an AMI. Next, Option B (correct) selects an appropriate instance type based on performance needs. After the instance type, Option C (correct) configures network settings and security groups to ensure proper connectivity and access control. Finally, Option D (correct) launches the instance and installs the application. Incorrect ordering, such as launching before configuring networking, would result in an inaccessible instance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_3_88',
  'A solutions architect is designing a highly available and scalable web application on Amazon EC2. Order the architectural steps to achieve this.',
  'ordering',
  '["Launch Amazon EC2 instances across multiple Availability Zones.","Place the EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB).","Configure an Auto Scaling Group (ASG) to manage instance scaling and health.","Implement health checks on the ALB and ASG to automatically replace unhealthy instances."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To achieve high availability, EC2 instances should first be launched across multiple Availability Zones (Option A). These instances are then placed behind an Application Load Balancer (Option B) for traffic distribution. An Auto Scaling Group (Option C) is configured to manage the desired number of instances and automatically scale based on demand. Finally, health checks (Option D) are implemented on both the ALB and ASG to ensure only healthy instances serve traffic and unhealthy ones are replaced.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_3',
  'medium'
);

