INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_82',
  'A monolithic application''s order processing logic is causing bottlenecks and needs to be decoupled into microservices using an event-driven architecture. The solutions architect has decided to use Amazon SQS for reliable message queuing and AWS Lambda for serverless processing. The team needs a clear sequence of steps to implement this pattern. Order the following steps to implement an event-driven architecture using Amazon SQS and AWS Lambda.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon SQS queue to store incoming order messages.","Configure an AWS Lambda function to process messages from the SQS queue.","Subscribe the Lambda function to the SQS queue as an event source.","Modify the monolithic application to send order messages to the SQS queue instead of directly invoking processing logic."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step in this event-driven pattern is to establish the messaging channel. Option A (correct) creates an Amazon SQS queue. Next, Option B (correct) configures an AWS Lambda function to handle the message processing. After the queue and function are ready, Option C (correct) subscribes the Lambda function to the SQS queue, linking them together. Finally, Option D (correct) modifies the source application to send messages to the SQS queue, completing the decoupling. Incorrect ordering would mean the queue or function isn''t ready when needed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_83',
  'A company has a legacy batch processing job that performs multiple sequential data transformations, currently running on an on-premises server using cron jobs. The solutions architect wants to modernize this workflow using serverless technologies to improve reliability, scalability, and reduce operational overhead. AWS Step Functions and AWS Lambda have been chosen for this task. Order the following steps to modernize a batch processing job using AWS Step Functions and AWS Lambda.',
  'ordering',
  '["Break down the batch processing job into smaller, independent steps, each implemented as an AWS Lambda function.","Define an AWS Step Functions state machine that orchestrates the execution order and logic of the Lambda functions.","Configure a trigger (e.g., Amazon EventBridge schedule) to initiate the Step Functions state machine at the required intervals.","Test the entire Step Functions workflow and deploy it to replace the legacy cron job."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Modernizing a batch job with Step Functions and Lambda begins with modularization. Option A (correct) involves breaking down the job into individual Lambda functions. Once the functions are defined, Option B (correct) defines an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate these functions. After the state machine is defined, Option C (correct) configures a trigger to initiate the workflow. Finally, Option D (correct) tests and deploys the complete workflow. Incorrect ordering would mean the functions aren''t ready or the state machine isn''t defined before attempting to trigger.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_84',
  'An application needs to broadcast notifications to multiple types of subscribers, including email, SMS, and other AWS services (e.g., AWS Lambda). The solutions architect needs to implement a publish-subscribe messaging pattern to achieve this decoupling and flexible notification delivery. Amazon SNS has been chosen for its fan-out capabilities. Order the following steps to implement a publish-subscribe messaging pattern using Amazon SNS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon SNS topic that will serve as the central communication channel.","Create subscriptions to the SNS topic for various endpoints, such as email addresses, SMS numbers, and AWS Lambda functions.","Modify the application to publish messages to the Amazon SNS topic.","Test the notification system by publishing a message to the SNS topic and verifying delivery to all subscribed endpoints."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step in a publish-subscribe pattern is to create the central topic. Option A (correct) creates an Amazon SNS topic. Next, Option B (correct) creates subscriptions to this topic for all desired endpoints. After subscriptions are established, Option C (correct) modifies the application to publish messages to the SNS topic. Finally, Option D (correct) tests the entire system to ensure messages are delivered correctly. Incorrect ordering would mean the topic or subscriptions aren''t ready when the application tries to publish.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_85',
  'A company wants to modernize its existing web application to be highly available, scalable, and resilient using containers. The solutions architect has decided to migrate the application to Amazon ECS, leveraging an Application Load Balancer for traffic distribution and Auto Scaling for dynamic capacity management. Order the following steps to migrate a web application to Amazon ECS with Auto Scaling and a load balancer.',
  'ordering',
  '["Containerize the web application and push its Docker image to an Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) repository.","Define an Amazon ECS Task Definition for the application, specifying container images, ports, and resource requirements.","Create an Amazon ECS Service to run and maintain the desired number of tasks, integrated with an Application Load Balancer (ALB).","Configure Amazon ECS Service Auto Scaling policies to dynamically adjust the number of tasks based on demand."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The migration process begins with preparing the application for containers. Option A (correct) involves containerizing the application and pushing the image to ECR. Next, Option B (correct) defines an Amazon ECS Task Definition, which describes how the container should run. After the Task Definition, Option C (correct) creates an Amazon ECS Service, integrating it with an Application Load Balancer for traffic distribution. Finally, Option D (correct) configures ECS Service Auto Scaling to handle varying loads. Incorrect ordering would mean the container image isn''t ready or the Task Definition isn''t defined before attempting to deploy the service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_86',
  'A development team needs to build a new RESTful API without managing any servers, aiming for maximum agility and cost-effectiveness. The solutions architect has chosen Amazon API Gateway for API management and AWS Lambda for serverless compute. A clear sequence of steps is required to implement this serverless API. Order the following steps to implement a serverless API with Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Lambda function to implement the API''s business logic.","Define an Amazon API Gateway REST API endpoint, specifying resources and HTTP methods.","Integrate the API Gateway method with the AWS Lambda function as the backend.","Configure authorization (e.g., IAM, Cognito, Lambda Authorizer) and deploy the API Gateway to a stage."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The implementation of a serverless API starts with the backend logic. Option A (correct) creates the AWS Lambda function that will execute the API''s business logic. Next, Option B (correct) defines the Amazon API Gateway REST API endpoint, including its resources and methods. After the API structure is defined, Option C (correct) integrates the API Gateway method with the Lambda function, linking the frontend to the backend. Finally, Option D (correct) configures authorization and deploys the API to a stage, making it accessible. Incorrect ordering would mean the Lambda function isn''t ready or the API Gateway isn''t defined before integration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_87',
  'A company needs to build a flexible data ingestion pipeline that can receive events from various SaaS applications and route them to different AWS services for processing. The solutions architect wants to use a serverless, event-driven approach with Amazon EventBridge to achieve this decoupling and routing. Order the following steps to implement a data ingestion pipeline using Amazon EventBridge.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon EventBridge event bus to receive and route events.","Define rules on the event bus to filter incoming events based on patterns (e.g., source, detail type).","Configure targets for each rule, specifying the AWS service (e.g., Lambda, SQS, SNS) that should process the filtered events.","Send events from source applications or services to the EventBridge event bus."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step in setting up an EventBridge pipeline is to create the central event bus. Option A (correct) creates an Amazon EventBridge event bus. Next, Option B (correct) defines rules on the event bus to filter and match incoming events. After rules are defined, Option C (correct) configures targets for each rule, specifying where the filtered events should be sent. Finally, Option D (correct) sends events to the EventBridge event bus, initiating the pipeline. Incorrect ordering would mean the event bus or rules aren''t ready when events are sent.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_88',
  'A large enterprise is adopting a microservices architecture for a complex new application, requiring advanced container orchestration capabilities, high scalability, and fine-grained control over the underlying infrastructure. The solutions architect has chosen Amazon EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service) for this deployment. The team needs a clear sequence of steps to set up the EKS environment and deploy their microservices. Order the following steps to implement a microservices architecture with Amazon EKS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon EKS cluster, specifying the Kubernetes version and networking configuration.","Configure `kubectl` to connect to the newly created EKS cluster.","Deploy Kubernetes manifests (Deployments, Services, Ingresses) for the microservices to the EKS cluster.","Configure monitoring and logging for the EKS cluster and deployed microservices using Amazon CloudWatch or Prometheus."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step in setting up an EKS environment is to provision the cluster itself. Option A (correct) creates the Amazon EKS cluster. Next, Option B (correct) configures `kubectl` to interact with the cluster. After `kubectl` is configured, Option C (correct) deploys the Kubernetes manifests for the microservices. Finally, Option D (correct) configures monitoring and logging for the cluster and applications. Incorrect ordering, such as deploying manifests before `kubectl` is configured, would be impossible.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_89',
  'A company wants to implement an event-driven architecture to decouple components of an existing application using Amazon EventBridge. Order the steps to configure this.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the event source that will publish events to EventBridge.","Create an EventBridge rule to match specific event patterns.","Configure the target AWS service (e.g., AWS Lambda, Amazon SQS) to be invoked by the rule.","Test the end-to-end event flow to ensure proper functionality."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step is to define the event source (Option A) that will send events to EventBridge. Next, an EventBridge rule (Option B) is created to specify which events to capture based on their patterns. Then, the target AWS service (Option C) is configured to be invoked when a rule matches an event. Finally, the entire event flow is tested (Option D) to verify correct operation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_90',
  'A solutions architect needs to decouple a monolithic application''s components using Amazon SQS for asynchronous communication. Order the steps to implement this decoupling.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon SQS queue to hold messages.","Configure the message producer component to send messages to the SQS queue.","Configure the message consumer component to poll and retrieve messages from the SQS queue.","Implement logic within the consumer to process the retrieved messages."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step is to create an Amazon SQS queue (Option A) which will act as the buffer. Next, the producer component is configured to send messages (Option B) to this queue. Then, the consumer component is configured to poll and retrieve messages (Option C) from the queue. Finally, the consumer implements the logic (Option D) to process these messages.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_91',
  'A development team wants to create a serverless workflow to orchestrate multiple AWS Lambda functions using AWS Step Functions. Order the steps to set up this workflow.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the state machine using Amazon States Language (ASL).","Implement the individual AWS Lambda functions that will be part of the workflow.","Test the execution of the state machine with sample inputs.","Monitor the workflow executions and troubleshoot any failures."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Before defining the workflow, the individual AWS Lambda functions (Option B) that perform the actual work must be implemented. Next, the state machine is defined (Option A) using ASL to orchestrate these functions. After definition, the workflow execution is tested (Option C) with sample inputs. Finally, the executions are monitored (Option D) to ensure proper operation and troubleshoot issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_92',
  'A solutions architect needs to deploy a containerized web application using Amazon ECS with the Fargate launch type. Order the typical steps for deploying the application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon ECS cluster.","Define an ECS task definition that specifies the container image and resources.","Create an ECS service to maintain the desired number of tasks.","Deploy the tasks to the ECS cluster using the service."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The deployment process starts with creating an Amazon ECS cluster (Option A) to host the containers. Next, an ECS task definition (Option B) is defined to describe how the application''s containers should run. Then, an ECS service (Option C) is created to manage the desired number of tasks. Finally, the tasks are deployed (Option D) to the cluster via the service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_93',
  'An organization is considering modernizing a monolithic application by adopting a microservices architecture. Order the conceptual steps involved in this modernization approach.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify bounded contexts within the monolith suitable for extraction.","Extract and deploy individual services independently.","Refactor communication patterns between services to be asynchronous and resilient.","Gradually replace monolithic components with new microservices."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}',
  'The modernization process begins by identifying bounded contexts (Option A) within the monolith that can become independent services. Next, monolithic components are gradually replaced (Option D) with new microservices, often using patterns like the Strangler Fig. As services are extracted, they are deployed independently (Option B). Finally, communication patterns (Option C) between these new services are refactored to be more cloud-native, asynchronous, and resilient.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_45_m_45_4_94',
  'A solutions architect needs to host a static website on AWS in a cost-effective and highly available manner. Order the steps for setting up a static website using Amazon S3.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon S3 bucket.","Upload all static website files (HTML, CSS, JS, images) to the S3 bucket.","Enable static website hosting for the S3 bucket.","Configure a bucket policy to allow public read access to the website content."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step is to create an Amazon S3 bucket (Option A) which will store the website content. Next, all static website files (Option B) are uploaded to this bucket. Then, static website hosting (Option C) is enabled for the bucket. Finally, a bucket policy (Option D) is configured to grant public read access, making the website accessible.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain45_9',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_45_4',
  'medium'
);

